00:33:32.220 --> 00:33:33.063
Here's the deal.

00:33:34.290 --> 00:33:36.900
When acetylcholine is
released from nucleus basalis,

00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:39.450
the neurons are there in
the base of the brain,

00:33:39.450 --> 00:33:41.040
but they extend axons,

00:33:41.040 --> 00:33:43.110
which are like little wires
elsewhere in the brain.

00:33:43.110 --> 00:33:45.060
And when acetylcholine is released,

00:33:45.060 --> 00:33:47.730
it tends to be released at
particular locations in the brain

00:33:47.730 --> 00:33:49.620
that are associated with whatever activity

00:33:49.620 --> 00:33:50.520
we happen to be doing.

00:33:50.520 --> 00:33:52.380
So if I'm doing Wordle,

00:33:52.380 --> 00:33:54.240
here I'm talking about it
as if I've ever done it,

00:33:54.240 --> 00:33:56.040
doing a crossword puzzle or Wordle,

00:33:56.040 --> 00:33:58.080
well the neurons that were
involved in trying to figure out

00:33:58.080 --> 00:34:00.930
the solution to that Wordle or
crossword puzzle are active,

00:34:00.930 --> 00:34:03.660
and then acetylcholine is
released from the little wires,

00:34:03.660 --> 00:34:06.540
the little endings of these
cells in nucleus basalis.

00:34:06.540 --> 00:34:09.510
And all of a sudden those
neural circuits get a boost.

00:34:09.510 --> 00:34:12.117
They become more active,
and believe it or not,

00:34:12.117 --> 00:34:14.670
our ability to perform
that crossword puzzle

00:34:14.670 --> 00:34:16.160
or at least focus on that Wordle

00:34:16.160 --> 00:34:19.080
or crossword puzzle gets enhanced.

00:34:19.080 --> 00:34:21.780
It literally increases
our attention for that

00:34:21.780 --> 00:34:23.820
and not anything else is enhanced.

00:34:23.820 --> 00:34:26.010
So it's literally like a neurochemical

00:34:26.010 --> 00:34:27.840
attentional spotlight.

00:34:27.840 --> 00:34:31.500
Nicotine increases
acetylcholine and thereby focus

00:34:31.500 --> 00:34:33.480
and concentration and mental performance,

00:34:33.480 --> 00:34:36.060
not by changing the neural circuits

00:34:36.060 --> 00:34:37.800
that are activated per se,

00:34:37.800 --> 00:34:40.980
but rather by making more acetylcholine

00:34:40.980 --> 00:34:43.620
available at those release sites.

00:34:43.620 --> 00:34:46.380
So it's as if the
spotlight got more intense,

00:34:46.380 --> 00:34:49.380
the highlighter is more intense
than it would be otherwise.

00:34:49.380 --> 00:34:51.180
And I should also mention because

00:34:51.180 --> 00:34:52.830
of the so-called pharmacokinetics,

00:34:52.830 --> 00:34:55.980
the time course in which
nicotine has its effects,

00:34:55.980 --> 00:34:57.270
which are pretty short lived,

00:34:57.270 --> 00:34:58.770
we'll talk about those in a moment,

00:34:58.770 --> 00:35:01.080
this enhancement in cognitive performance

00:35:01.080 --> 00:35:03.420
and attention, it's going
to be very transient,

00:35:03.420 --> 00:35:06.870
probably on the order of
about 30, maybe 45 minutes,

00:35:06.870 --> 00:35:08.010
the half life of nicotine,

00:35:08.010 --> 00:35:09.230
depending on how it's ingested and whether

00:35:09.230 --> 00:35:10.410
or not you have food in the gut

00:35:10.410 --> 00:35:12.120
and what else is in the
bloodstream, et cetera,

00:35:12.120 --> 00:35:14.490
it's going to be anywhere
from one to two hours,

00:35:14.490 --> 00:35:16.380
but typically the effects
of nicotine will come on

00:35:16.380 --> 00:35:18.960
in about two to 15 minutes
as I mentioned before,

00:35:18.960 --> 00:35:22.470
and then will last anywhere
from about 30 to 45 minutes.

00:35:22.470 --> 00:35:24.030
This is why in the old days,

00:35:24.030 --> 00:35:26.460
and still to some extent in
certain areas of the world,

00:35:26.460 --> 00:35:28.260
but less so in the United States

00:35:28.260 --> 00:35:30.120
and certainly in Europe as well,

00:35:30.120 --> 00:35:32.700
we don't see quite as many
people smoking cigarettes,

00:35:32.700 --> 00:35:34.890
for reasons we can discuss later.

00:35:34.890 --> 00:35:37.170
But you would see these chain smokers

00:35:37.170 --> 00:35:39.810
who are trying to maintain constant levels

00:35:39.810 --> 00:35:42.030
of nicotine in their
brain and bloodstream.

00:35:42.030 --> 00:35:44.820
Now perhaps they didn't know that nicotine

00:35:44.820 --> 00:35:46.350
has this one to two hour half life,

00:35:46.350 --> 00:35:49.710
but they could sense
no doubt the cognitive

00:35:49.710 --> 00:35:51.000
and the physical effects of nicotine,

00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:52.890
including this cognitive
enhancement effect

00:35:52.890 --> 00:35:55.290
and highlighting of
neural circuits effect.

00:35:55.290 --> 00:35:57.630
And they would notice they
would smoke a cigarette

00:35:57.630 --> 00:36:00.750
and then for the next five
to 45 minutes have heightened

00:36:00.750 --> 00:36:02.130
focus and then they
would start to drop off.

00:36:02.130 --> 00:36:03.180
So they smoke another cigarette.

00:36:03.180 --> 00:36:04.013
So in other words,

00:36:04.013 --> 00:36:07.350
they're trying to maintain
a constant level of nicotine

00:36:07.350 --> 00:36:09.330
for whatever activities
they needed to perform.

00:36:09.330 --> 00:36:10.800
Obviously chain smoking

00:36:10.800 --> 00:36:13.590
because of the terrible
effects of smoking,

00:36:13.590 --> 00:36:14.547
I'll talk about those terrible effects,

00:36:14.547 --> 00:36:17.940
but I'm sure you've heard
of them before, cancer,

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:20.670
depletion of just about
every organ and body tissue

00:36:20.670 --> 00:36:22.830
to the point that it
can actually be measured

00:36:22.830 --> 00:36:24.720
how many years of your
life you're peeling off

00:36:24.720 --> 00:36:27.360
in terms of lifespan and
health span by smoking.

00:36:27.360 --> 00:36:31.410
Well the terrible effects
of smoking are indisputable,

00:36:31.410 --> 00:36:35.460
but the positive effects of
nicotine on this circuitry,

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:37.830
part of the reason why
people would chain smoke

00:36:37.830 --> 00:36:40.500
in the first place rather
than get one big peak

00:36:40.500 --> 00:36:42.750
of concentration and focus
and then just let it disappear

00:36:42.750 --> 00:36:44.013
after 45 minutes.

00:36:45.180 --> 00:36:47.460
So what we have is a
scenario where dopamine

00:36:47.460 --> 00:36:49.590
is going up in the mesolimbic pathway.

00:36:49.590 --> 00:36:52.410
That's why smoking or ingesting
nicotine in any other way

00:36:52.410 --> 00:36:54.690
feels good and makes us feel motivated.

00:36:54.690 --> 00:36:56.280
And then the increase in acetylcholine,

00:36:56.280 --> 00:36:58.620
especially from nucleus basalis
in the front of the brain,

00:36:58.620 --> 00:37:01.380
is the reason why it
can increase our ability

00:37:01.380 --> 00:37:04.260
to focus on particular types of endeavors,

00:37:04.260 --> 00:37:05.850
particular mental work that we're doing,

00:37:05.850 --> 00:37:07.710
or maybe even particular physical work.

00:37:07.710 --> 00:37:10.200
Although I should mention
anytime I'm pairing the words

00:37:10.200 --> 00:37:11.820
nicotine and physical work,

00:37:11.820 --> 00:37:13.740
it's obvious that because of the ways

00:37:13.740 --> 00:37:16.260
that smoking impairs lung function,

00:37:16.260 --> 00:37:18.410
those two things really
run counter to one another.

00:37:18.410 --> 00:37:19.243
In other words,

00:37:19.243 --> 00:37:21.990
if you are thinking about
ingesting nicotine through smoking

00:37:21.990 --> 00:37:24.713
or vaping in order to
improve physical performance,

00:37:24.713 --> 00:37:26.460
that's a terrible idea.

00:37:26.460 --> 00:37:28.440
The logic isn't there
and the health detriments

00:37:28.440 --> 00:37:29.273
are certainly there.

00:37:29.273 --> 00:37:32.670
The third neurochemical pathway
that's strongly activated

00:37:32.670 --> 00:37:36.050
when nicotine is brought into
the central nervous system

00:37:36.050 --> 00:37:38.010
into the brain is epinephrine,

00:37:38.010 --> 00:37:39.330
or in particular norepinephrine,

00:37:39.330 --> 00:37:40.620
which is related to epinephrine.

00:37:40.620 --> 00:37:43.230
Now, earlier I said epinephrine
is the same as adrenaline.

00:37:43.230 --> 00:37:44.310
That's still true.

00:37:44.310 --> 00:37:46.320
Norepinephrine is closely
related to epinephrine,

00:37:46.320 --> 00:37:47.460
and for today's discussion,

00:37:47.460 --> 00:37:48.900
we're going to use them interchangeably.

00:37:48.900 --> 00:37:51.210
Although I realize as I say that

00:37:51.210 --> 00:37:53.250
that the medical students
and some biology students are

00:37:53.250 --> 00:37:54.900
probably going to have a minor seizure

00:37:54.900 --> 00:37:57.120
when I lump norepinephrine
and epinephrine.

00:37:57.120 --> 00:38:00.000
I don't do that to be
too much of a lumper,

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:01.860
in science, we talk about
lumpers and splitters.

00:38:01.860 --> 00:38:04.080
Lumpers are people that like
to oversimplify a little bit,

00:38:04.080 --> 00:38:05.970
splitters are people that
really like to detail.

00:38:05.970 --> 00:38:07.890
You'll see a lot of
splitters on social media

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:09.390
from time to time, they'll say, wait,

00:38:09.390 --> 00:38:13.140
you didn't mention the alpha
two beta six receptor yet.

00:38:13.140 --> 00:38:14.610
Okay look, I get it.

00:38:14.610 --> 00:38:17.130
And I am all for having
splitters in the room,

00:38:17.130 --> 00:38:18.930
but for sake of today's discussion

00:38:18.930 --> 00:38:22.020
and for ease of digestibility
of some of this,

00:38:22.020 --> 00:38:23.580
just want to point out
that norepinephrine,

00:38:23.580 --> 00:38:24.840
epinephrine, and adrenaline,

00:38:24.840 --> 00:38:27.990
I'm going to treat as a common
pool of similar, in fact,

00:38:27.990 --> 00:38:31.740
very similar molecules that
all have the same net effect,

00:38:31.740 --> 00:38:33.240
at least in the context of this discussion

00:38:33.240 --> 00:38:34.650
and that's to increase levels

00:38:34.650 --> 00:38:37.050
of alertness, energy, and arousal.

00:38:37.050 --> 00:38:39.720
And the way that nicotine
accomplishes those increases in

00:38:39.720 --> 00:38:43.170
alertness and arousal and
energy within the brain is by

00:38:43.170 --> 00:38:45.030
triggering the release of
no epinephrine from a little

00:38:45.030 --> 00:38:46.440
cluster of neurons in
the back of the brain

00:38:46.440 --> 00:38:48.480
called locus coeruleus,

00:38:48.480 --> 00:38:52.620
tiny cluster of neurons that
offers up, or I should say has,

00:38:52.620 --> 00:38:55.020
because they're always there from birth

00:38:55.020 --> 00:38:58.080
has these little wires,
these axons that extend many,

00:38:58.080 --> 00:38:59.820
many places in the brain, not every place,

00:38:59.820 --> 00:39:01.050
but virtually every place

00:39:01.050 --> 00:39:03.480
and can sprinkler the
brain with norepinephrine,

00:39:03.480 --> 00:39:05.520
essentially serve as a wake up signal,

00:39:05.520 --> 00:39:07.320
elevating levels of energy.

00:39:07.320 --> 00:39:09.728
And when that combines
with the acetylcholine

00:39:09.728 --> 00:39:14.070
from nucleus basalis, which
causes attentional spotlighting,

00:39:14.070 --> 00:39:16.530
increases in concentration and focus,

00:39:16.530 --> 00:39:19.137
and with the feel good
properties of dopamine

00:39:19.137 --> 00:39:21.630
and the motivating properties
of dopamine released

00:39:21.630 --> 00:39:23.760
from the mesolimbic reward pathway.

00:39:23.760 --> 00:39:27.420
Now you can start to get
a picture of why nicotine

00:39:27.420 --> 00:39:30.180
is such a powerful molecule.

00:39:30.180 --> 00:39:32.070
It's making people feel
motivated and good.

00:39:32.070 --> 00:39:35.820
It's making people feel focused
and it makes people feel

00:39:35.820 --> 00:39:38.490
alert when they would otherwise
feel a little bit sleepy.

00:39:38.490 --> 00:39:41.670
So this is a really
powerful compound, in fact,

00:39:41.670 --> 00:39:43.980
going back to our earlier
discussion about focus

00:39:43.980 --> 00:39:45.150
and some tools for focus,

00:39:45.150 --> 00:39:46.530
and I encourage you if you're interested

00:39:46.530 --> 00:39:48.090
to please check out the episode on focus,

00:39:48.090 --> 00:39:49.770
there are a number of different tools

00:39:49.770 --> 00:39:51.690
and protocols there to increase focus.

00:39:51.690 --> 00:39:56.580
But here we are talking
about one molecule, nicotine,

00:39:56.580 --> 00:39:59.220
found in plants like tomatoes and potatoes

00:39:59.220 --> 00:40:00.810
and the tobacco plant.

00:40:00.810 --> 00:40:03.870
And it can be synthesized
in a laboratory and ingested

00:40:03.870 --> 00:40:07.350
through a patch or a gum or even a pill

00:40:07.350 --> 00:40:09.480
or a toothpick dipped in nicotine.

00:40:09.480 --> 00:40:14.480
One molecule that can trigger
activation of all the circuits

00:40:14.970 --> 00:40:19.140
for focus and motivation
in one fell swoop.

00:40:19.140 --> 00:40:22.170
That is remarkable. That
is absolutely remarkable.

00:40:22.170 --> 00:40:23.190
And here we haven't even touched on

00:40:23.190 --> 00:40:25.200
some of the psychological
components of focus, right?

00:40:25.200 --> 00:40:26.640
Whether or not we're
interested in something,

00:40:26.640 --> 00:40:28.920
whether or not we're
excited about it or not.

00:40:28.920 --> 00:40:31.380
This is a very, very powerful system.

00:40:31.380 --> 00:40:33.030
So powerful in fact,

00:40:33.030 --> 00:40:34.890
that I think we can really place nicotine

00:40:34.890 --> 00:40:37.020
right up there at the top,

00:40:37.020 --> 00:40:40.080
right next to caffeine as the molecule

00:40:40.080 --> 00:40:44.280
that has fundamentally
changed human evolution,

00:40:44.280 --> 00:40:46.770
human consciousness, and human experience.

00:40:46.770 --> 00:40:49.380
Even if you're somebody who's
never ingested nicotine,

00:40:49.380 --> 00:40:50.820
this absolutely has to be true

00:40:50.820 --> 00:40:52.740
because you have these
nicotinic receptors,

00:40:52.740 --> 00:40:55.560
which is to say that acetylcholine
that's naturally released

00:40:55.560 --> 00:40:58.920
without any external trigger
within your brain and body,

00:40:58.920 --> 00:40:59.753
or I should say

00:40:59.753 --> 00:41:02.340
without any trigger from
nicotine in particular

00:41:02.340 --> 00:41:05.520
is binding these nicotinic
acetylcholine receptors

00:41:05.520 --> 00:41:07.830
and is creating these effects
in your brain and body,

00:41:07.830 --> 00:41:09.480
just absolutely staggering.

00:41:09.480 --> 00:41:12.750
Now earlier I mentioned the
appetite suppressing and indeed

00:41:12.750 --> 00:41:15.630
metabolism increasing effects of nicotine.

00:41:15.630 --> 00:41:18.210
And while that's a fairly niche component

00:41:18.210 --> 00:41:20.580
of what nicotine does, I
mean, it's an important one,

00:41:20.580 --> 00:41:22.320
but it's not the major reason

00:41:22.320 --> 00:41:23.937
why most people consume nicotine.

00:41:23.937 --> 00:41:25.980
I'd like to take a moment
and talk about that now,

00:41:25.980 --> 00:41:27.960
because we are in the
brain and we're talking

00:41:27.960 --> 00:41:29.277
about the effects of
nicotine in the brain.

00:41:29.277 --> 00:41:31.260
And so it seems to me the appropriate time

00:41:31.260 --> 00:41:32.160
to talk about this.

00:41:32.160 --> 00:41:34.530
Now we can have this
conversation about nicotine

00:41:34.530 --> 00:41:36.570
and appetite and
metabolism in a very simple

00:41:36.570 --> 00:41:38.070
and straightforward way.

00:41:38.070 --> 00:41:42.240
If you'd like to learn more
about the biology of metabolism

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:43.950
and appetite and how those things

00:41:43.950 --> 00:41:46.230
are mediated by neural components,

00:41:46.230 --> 00:41:48.240
so not just stuff like
your liver, et cetera,

00:41:48.240 --> 00:41:49.260
we have episodes on that,

00:41:49.260 --> 00:41:52.200
but really the neural components
of hunger and appetite.

00:41:52.200 --> 00:41:53.550
I encourage you to check out our episode

00:41:53.550 --> 00:41:55.290
on hunger and appetite.

00:41:55.290 --> 00:41:58.230
But in that episode, we had a discussion.

00:41:58.230 --> 00:42:01.290
And it's one that I'll just
briefly summarize now that you

00:42:01.290 --> 00:42:03.330
have a collection of neurons
that sits right above the roof

00:42:03.330 --> 00:42:06.856
of your mouth or so
called the hypothalamus.

00:42:06.856 --> 00:42:08.580
Hypo means below and
thalamus is right above it,

00:42:08.580 --> 00:42:09.540
hypothalamus.

00:42:09.540 --> 00:42:12.240
So the small collection of
neurons in the hypothalamus

00:42:13.220 --> 00:42:15.600
to a number of different
things related to sex behavior,

00:42:15.600 --> 00:42:18.660
aggression, mediating the
temperature of your body,

00:42:18.660 --> 00:42:22.140
et cetera, but also appetite
and suppressing appetite.

00:42:22.140 --> 00:42:23.250
And within the hypothalamus,

00:42:23.250 --> 00:42:26.160
there's a compact collection
of these little neurons,

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:29.010
which we referred to as
the POMC, P-O-M-C neurons.

00:42:29.010 --> 00:42:30.000
And the name comes from the fact

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:31.230
that they express certain peptides.

00:42:31.230 --> 00:42:32.760
And we won't get into that now,

00:42:32.760 --> 00:42:37.230
but the POMC neurons have
a very profound impact on

00:42:37.230 --> 00:42:38.670
whether or not you feel hungry

00:42:38.670 --> 00:42:41.040
or whether or not you do not feel hungry,

00:42:41.040 --> 00:42:43.410
whether or not your
appetite is suppressed.

00:42:43.410 --> 00:42:45.570
It turns out that when nicotine
gets into the bloodstream

00:42:45.570 --> 00:42:46.860
and then into the brain,

00:42:46.860 --> 00:42:50.640
some of that nicotine binds to nicotinic

00:42:50.640 --> 00:42:53.850
alpha four beta two containing
nicotinic receptors.

00:42:53.850 --> 00:42:55.200
Again, these sub units of receptors,

00:42:55.200 --> 00:42:56.490
but basically the nicotine binds some

00:42:56.490 --> 00:42:58.080
of those parking spots, parks there.

00:42:58.080 --> 00:42:59.070
And as a consequence,

00:42:59.070 --> 00:43:03.450
these POMC neurons increase
their electrical activity

00:43:03.450 --> 00:43:05.730
and appetite is suppressed.

00:43:05.730 --> 00:43:09.090
And that's because the POMC
neurons have outputs to various

00:43:09.090 --> 00:43:11.670
areas of the brain and
body controlling everything

00:43:11.670 --> 00:43:15.210
from how full we feel to whether or not

00:43:15.210 --> 00:43:17.400
our blood sugar goes up or down,

00:43:17.400 --> 00:43:20.160
which can impact our hunger,
and believe it or not,

00:43:20.160 --> 00:43:22.650
whether or not we have a
tendency to want to move the jaw

00:43:22.650 --> 00:43:24.750
of our mouth in order to chew food.

00:43:24.750 --> 00:43:25.830
Yes, believe it or not,

00:43:25.830 --> 00:43:28.290
the neural circuitry
associated with appetite and

00:43:28.290 --> 00:43:30.750
suppression of appetite actually dictates

00:43:30.750 --> 00:43:32.700
whether or not you prefer to,

00:43:32.700 --> 00:43:35.460
or I should say are more
biased or less biased

00:43:35.460 --> 00:43:38.460
to moving your mouth, that is chewing.

00:43:38.460 --> 00:43:40.680
Which makes perfect
sense when you hear it.

00:43:40.680 --> 00:43:41.760
One way to suppress appetite

00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:44.850
is to sew the jaw shut neurally,

00:43:44.850 --> 00:43:46.350
or at least make it less likely

00:43:46.350 --> 00:43:48.720
to open your mouth and put food in it.

00:43:48.720 --> 00:43:50.220
Actually that reminded me of a story.

00:43:50.220 --> 00:43:52.620
I'll just interrupt myself
to tell a brief story that

00:43:52.620 --> 00:43:55.230
there's a famous Nobel Laureate
who won the Nobel Prize

00:43:55.230 --> 00:43:57.908
for something totally
distant from appetite.

00:43:57.908 --> 00:44:00.570
But once turned to a friend
of mine at a meeting and said,

00:44:00.570 --> 00:44:05.570
I discovered the biological
mechanism for losing weight.

00:44:06.480 --> 00:44:08.493
And my friend said, well of course,

00:44:09.750 --> 00:44:11.370
ingest fewer calories than you burn.

00:44:11.370 --> 00:44:12.300
Calories in, calories out,

00:44:12.300 --> 00:44:13.972
fundamental rule of thermodynamics.

00:44:13.972 --> 00:44:16.230
And basically the fundamental
rule of weight loss,

00:44:16.230 --> 00:44:18.120
weight gain, or weight maintenance.

00:44:18.120 --> 00:44:19.590
And he said, no,

00:44:19.590 --> 00:44:21.480
it's actually the gene

00:44:21.480 --> 00:44:23.940
that controls whether or
not you open your mouth.

00:44:23.940 --> 00:44:25.830
Now he was making a very nerdy joke.

00:44:25.830 --> 00:44:27.570
So if you didn't register that as a joke,

00:44:27.570 --> 00:44:30.930
that's about as funny as
neuroscience or biology jokes get.

00:44:30.930 --> 00:44:31.830
There are a couple funnier ones,

00:44:31.830 --> 00:44:33.660
but that one's kind of
considered on the funnier side.

00:44:33.660 --> 00:44:36.510
So this is why we're not
considered comedians.

00:44:36.510 --> 00:44:41.510
But the point of the matter is
that whether or not you crave

00:44:41.760 --> 00:44:44.580
or desire or impulsively
want to put things

00:44:44.580 --> 00:44:45.510
in your mouth and chew it,

00:44:45.510 --> 00:44:47.760
will actually dictate how
many calories that you eat.

00:44:47.760 --> 00:44:50.490
And so I find it remarkable
and indeed important to know

00:44:50.490 --> 00:44:52.890
that these POMC neurons
are actually inhibiting

00:44:52.890 --> 00:44:55.170
the opening and the movement
of the mouth for chewing.

00:44:55.170 --> 00:44:57.270
So when we smoke or
when we ingest nicotine

00:44:57.270 --> 00:44:58.470
in any other way,

00:44:58.470 --> 00:45:00.750
you activate these POMC
neurons, you suppress appetite,

00:45:00.750 --> 00:45:02.880
but in part you do that
by actually limiting

00:45:02.880 --> 00:45:04.320
the impulse to chew.

00:45:04.320 --> 00:45:05.940
Incredible, at least to me.

00:45:05.940 --> 00:45:08.130
Now in addition to limiting appetite

00:45:08.130 --> 00:45:11.370
by changing one's desire
to ingest food and chew it

00:45:11.370 --> 00:45:14.100
and actual craving of food by regulation

00:45:14.100 --> 00:45:15.420
of blood sugar, et cetera,

00:45:15.420 --> 00:45:19.530
there do seem to be some quite
direct effects of nicotine

00:45:19.530 --> 00:45:20.970
on metabolism,

00:45:20.970 --> 00:45:23.340
and the effects on
metabolism aren't enormous.

00:45:23.340 --> 00:45:25.110
These are increases in metabolism

00:45:25.110 --> 00:45:28.830
that are about 2% up to about 5%,

00:45:28.830 --> 00:45:30.750
but I want to emphasize that those

00:45:30.750 --> 00:45:33.930
are transient increases in metabolism.

00:45:33.930 --> 00:45:37.440
Nonetheless, people that
quit smoking often find

00:45:37.440 --> 00:45:39.810
that their appetite goes up,
they sometimes gain weight.

00:45:39.810 --> 00:45:41.940
They sometimes do not depending
on whether or not they

00:45:41.940 --> 00:45:44.370
offset that increase in
appetite with increased physical

00:45:44.370 --> 00:45:47.310
exercise or with decrease
food intake in other ways.

00:45:47.310 --> 00:45:49.080
But there does seem to
be this direct effect

00:45:49.080 --> 00:45:51.090
of ingesting nicotine on metabolism,

00:45:51.090 --> 00:45:52.470
which I find is interesting

00:45:52.470 --> 00:45:53.940
because if you look in the literature,

00:45:53.940 --> 00:45:57.090
one of the reasons why
people are reluctant

00:45:57.090 --> 00:46:00.540
to quit ingesting
nicotine, if for instance,

00:46:00.540 --> 00:46:04.650
they want to quit using the
delivery device to nicotine

00:46:04.650 --> 00:46:06.870
that's causing such problems
for their health like smoking

00:46:06.870 --> 00:46:10.740
or vaping or whether or
not they find themselves

00:46:10.740 --> 00:46:12.360
quote unquote addicted to,

00:46:12.360 --> 00:46:15.480
or have the habit of ingesting nicotine.

00:46:15.480 --> 00:46:19.080
In part, that's likely due
to be the dopamine effects,

00:46:19.080 --> 00:46:21.180
because dopamine is highly
reinforcing and rewarding.

00:46:21.180 --> 00:46:23.400
It feels good, so people
want to do more of it.

00:46:23.400 --> 00:46:26.550
But it's also that for many
people and here the data really

00:46:26.550 --> 00:46:29.640
point to the fact that a lot
of the younger female smokers

00:46:29.640 --> 00:46:33.930
or younger female vapers,
or when I say that,

00:46:33.930 --> 00:46:38.040
of course I mean younger
females that vape,

00:46:38.040 --> 00:46:39.750
are doing that because they like

00:46:39.750 --> 00:46:41.280
the appetite suppressing effects,

00:46:41.280 --> 00:46:43.350
which of course opens up
an entire conversation

00:46:43.350 --> 00:46:46.290
about the sociology of
body imagery, et cetera,

00:46:46.290 --> 00:46:48.210
a topic for a future podcast.

00:46:48.210 --> 00:46:52.530
So nicotine has certain effects
on the brain by virtue of

00:46:52.530 --> 00:46:54.390
the fact that nicotine binds

00:46:54.390 --> 00:46:56.400
these nicotinic acetylcholine receptors.

00:46:56.400 --> 00:46:58.110
And those receptors are found on some,

00:46:58.110 --> 00:47:00.240
but not all neural
circuits within the brain.

00:47:00.240 --> 00:47:02.550
And we talked about some of
them already, mesolimbic,

00:47:02.550 --> 00:47:04.380
the POMC neurons, et cetera.

00:47:04.380 --> 00:47:05.910
Now when we ingest nicotine,

00:47:05.910 --> 00:47:09.690
it goes from the bloodstream
to all the tissues

00:47:09.690 --> 00:47:10.770
and organs of the body.

00:47:10.770 --> 00:47:12.750
How does it do that? I mean, amazing.

00:47:12.750 --> 00:47:15.630
It can pass to everything,
the brain, the body,

00:47:15.630 --> 00:47:19.050
it does that because
nicotine is fat soluble.

00:47:19.050 --> 00:47:20.910
And now when anytime
people hear the word fat,

00:47:20.910 --> 00:47:23.700
they tend to think about
body fat, subcutaneous fat,

00:47:23.700 --> 00:47:25.860
or maybe they think about dietary fat.

00:47:25.860 --> 00:47:28.650
What I mean by fat soluble in
the context of nicotine being

00:47:28.650 --> 00:47:32.280
fat soluble is that the cells of your body

00:47:32.280 --> 00:47:35.670
have an outer layer,
so-called outer membrane.

00:47:35.670 --> 00:47:37.800
And it's made up of lipid, of fat,

00:47:37.800 --> 00:47:39.800
very particular types of lipids in fact.

00:47:40.800 --> 00:47:44.850
Nicotine has this remarkable
ability to move through

00:47:44.850 --> 00:47:46.680
that fatty tissue.

00:47:46.680 --> 00:47:50.370
Not all molecules have that
ability, but nicotine does.

00:47:50.370 --> 00:47:52.830
So it can move relatively
freely through the brain

00:47:52.830 --> 00:47:56.250
and body and relatively
freely from outside of cells,

00:47:56.250 --> 00:47:59.280
extracellular space to
intracellular space.

00:47:59.280 --> 00:48:01.290
So it can get into cells, it
can do that within the brain.

00:48:01.290 --> 00:48:02.340
We talked about those effects

00:48:02.340 --> 00:48:04.230
and it can do that within the body.

00:48:04.230 --> 00:48:06.300
Now anytime we're talking about the body,

00:48:06.300 --> 00:48:07.830
we can be talking about
any number of things,

00:48:07.830 --> 00:48:10.110
but today I'm going to
refer to the periphery

00:48:10.110 --> 00:48:11.880
and the body in more or less the same way,

00:48:11.880 --> 00:48:15.753
but keep in mind in the back
of your head, pun intended,

00:48:16.620 --> 00:48:20.130
you have your brain, your
eyes, and the spinal cord.

00:48:20.130 --> 00:48:22.770
And those three things make up
your central nervous system.

00:48:22.770 --> 00:48:24.720
The peripheral nervous
system and the periphery,

00:48:24.720 --> 00:48:26.130
which is the rest of your body,

00:48:26.130 --> 00:48:28.290
the contain your organs
and so forth outside

00:48:28.290 --> 00:48:29.580
of the nervous system,

00:48:29.580 --> 00:48:31.740
things like your liver and
your stomach, et cetera,

00:48:31.740 --> 00:48:34.590
that's what we're going to
talk about now because nicotine

00:48:34.590 --> 00:48:37.410
has profound effects on
the organs of the body

00:48:37.410 --> 00:48:38.820
that are separate from,

00:48:38.820 --> 00:48:41.580
but that occur in
parallel at the same time

00:48:41.580 --> 00:48:44.130
as the effects of nicotine on the brain.

00:48:44.130 --> 00:48:46.080
So let's talk about what
some of those effects are.

00:48:46.080 --> 00:48:48.030
When nicotine makes it into
the bloodstream, again,

00:48:48.030 --> 00:48:50.130
within two to 15 minutes of ingesting it,

00:48:50.130 --> 00:48:51.780
depending on the delivery device,

00:48:53.160 --> 00:48:55.650
your heart rate will increase,

00:48:55.650 --> 00:48:57.293
blood pressure will increase,

00:48:57.293 --> 00:49:00.120
and the contractibility
of the heart tissue

00:49:00.120 --> 00:49:01.920
will actually increase.

00:49:01.920 --> 00:49:05.580
So what that essentially
speaks to is an increase

00:49:05.580 --> 00:49:07.230
in so-called sympathetic tone.

00:49:07.230 --> 00:49:08.063
And when I say that,

00:49:08.063 --> 00:49:09.690
I don't mean an increase in sympathy

00:49:09.690 --> 00:49:11.463
for others of the emotional sort.

00:49:12.450 --> 00:49:15.750
What I mean is an increase
in the sympathetic activity

00:49:15.750 --> 00:49:18.060
of the sympathetic arm of
the autonomic nervous system,

00:49:18.060 --> 00:49:21.600
which is real mouthful
and mindful of ideas.

00:49:21.600 --> 00:49:24.810
But all you need to know is
that it's a generalized system

00:49:24.810 --> 00:49:28.650
that increases levels of
alertness and physical readiness.

00:49:28.650 --> 00:49:29.850
So it makes you ready for action,

00:49:29.850 --> 00:49:31.560
makes you ready for thought.

00:49:31.560 --> 00:49:33.510
It's balanced by a whole
other system called

00:49:33.510 --> 00:49:35.220
the parasympathetic nervous system,

00:49:35.220 --> 00:49:38.070
which is basically the so-called
rest and digest system,

00:49:38.070 --> 00:49:40.650
which is a system of neurons
and organs, et cetera,

00:49:40.650 --> 00:49:43.920
that put your body and your
brain into a state of not being

00:49:43.920 --> 00:49:47.250
able to think clearly, to
digest, and to fall asleep.

00:49:47.250 --> 00:49:49.650
So nicotine increases heart rate,

00:49:49.650 --> 00:49:51.600
blood pressure, and
contractibility of the heart.

00:49:51.600 --> 00:49:54.600
So it's going to cause
more blood flow in theory,

00:49:54.600 --> 00:49:58.080
although it also tends to
constrict blood vessels

00:49:58.080 --> 00:49:59.670
in many locations in the body.

00:49:59.670 --> 00:50:03.120
This explains the decrease
in penile girth effect

00:50:03.120 --> 00:50:04.560
of nicotine, in particular

00:50:04.560 --> 00:50:07.350
nicotine ingested by smoking or vaping.

00:50:07.350 --> 00:50:11.580
That's right, smoking and
vaping reduces penis size

00:50:11.580 --> 00:50:14.760
and also will have damaging effects

00:50:14.760 --> 00:50:18.180
on the blood lining endothelial tissue.

00:50:18.180 --> 00:50:21.120
So over time it actually is
impairing blood's ability to get

00:50:21.120 --> 00:50:23.280
to the penis chronically

00:50:23.280 --> 00:50:25.200
as well as to other organs of the body.

00:50:25.200 --> 00:50:27.840
But when people ingest nicotine acutely,

00:50:27.840 --> 00:50:30.300
and let's say they do
that by Nicorette patch

00:50:30.300 --> 00:50:32.460
or by toothpick dipped in nicotine,

00:50:32.460 --> 00:50:34.500
it will have some of these same effects,

00:50:34.500 --> 00:50:37.440
but when not smoking tobacco,

00:50:37.440 --> 00:50:39.330
when bringing nicotine
into the bloodstream

00:50:39.330 --> 00:50:40.830
through other mechanisms,

00:50:40.830 --> 00:50:43.500
many, if not all of the
disruption of the endothelial cell

00:50:43.500 --> 00:50:48.500
function can be bypassed, but
the effects on penile girth,

00:50:49.380 --> 00:50:52.080
the effects on reducing
blood flow to various tissues

00:50:52.080 --> 00:50:55.380
is still present during
the effects of nicotine,

00:50:55.380 --> 00:50:57.540
which as I mentioned last
about one to two hours.

00:50:57.540 --> 00:50:59.250
The half life is about one to two hours,

00:50:59.250 --> 00:51:00.810
depending on a number of factors,

00:51:00.810 --> 00:51:03.180
not interesting for today's discussion.

00:51:03.180 --> 00:51:05.490
So when nicotine gets
into the bloodstream,

00:51:05.490 --> 00:51:06.536
it's making us more alert,

00:51:06.536 --> 00:51:08.820
it's preparing our body for readiness.

00:51:08.820 --> 00:51:10.233
The heart is pumping harder.

00:51:11.400 --> 00:51:13.110
Epinephrine, that is adrenaline,

00:51:13.110 --> 00:51:14.790
is released from the adrenal glands,

00:51:14.790 --> 00:51:16.860
which right atop our kidneys.

00:51:16.860 --> 00:51:20.730
So everything is pointed
toward creating more readiness

00:51:20.730 --> 00:51:22.920
to move, more readiness to think.

00:51:22.920 --> 00:51:23.753
And again,

00:51:23.753 --> 00:51:25.560
this is happening in parallel
with all the effects of

00:51:25.560 --> 00:51:27.210
neurochemistry that are
happening with the brain

00:51:27.210 --> 00:51:29.730
that we talked about a few minutes ago.

00:51:29.730 --> 00:51:32.310
Now what's interesting about
nicotine is that while it

00:51:32.310 --> 00:51:34.500
causes this global increase in readiness

00:51:34.500 --> 00:51:37.230
and alertness and attention
and mood, et cetera,

00:51:37.230 --> 00:51:42.060
it also has the effect of
somewhat relaxing skeletal muscle.

00:51:42.060 --> 00:51:44.310
Now that might seem
counterintuitive to those of you out

00:51:44.310 --> 00:51:46.110
there that already know what I'm about

00:51:46.110 --> 00:51:49.560
to tell the rest of you who
didn't know it previously,

00:51:49.560 --> 00:51:51.870
that your muscles are able to

00:51:51.870 --> 00:51:56.870
contract because of the effects
of acetylcholine released

00:51:57.030 --> 00:52:01.620
from neurons in your spinal
cord that spit out acetylcholine

00:52:01.620 --> 00:52:03.963
onto the muscle and bind to what?

00:52:04.890 --> 00:52:06.210
Nicotinic acetylcholine receptors.

00:52:06.210 --> 00:52:07.498
Put into plain English,

00:52:07.498 --> 00:52:10.530
nicotinic receptors are also the ways

00:52:10.530 --> 00:52:12.750
in which your muscles can get activated.

00:52:12.750 --> 00:52:13.583
So therefore,

00:52:13.583 --> 00:52:15.330
why would it be that increasing nicotine

00:52:15.330 --> 00:52:17.790
would cause relaxation of the muscles?

00:52:17.790 --> 00:52:20.160
And that has to do with some
of the neural circuits that are

00:52:20.160 --> 00:52:22.530
upstream of the muscles and
has to do with a little bit

00:52:22.530 --> 00:52:24.840
of how the autonomic
nervous system is a range

00:52:24.840 --> 00:52:26.520
in terms of which receptors go where,

00:52:26.520 --> 00:52:29.550
a topic and kind of rabbit
hole of a conversation

00:52:29.550 --> 00:52:31.380
far too deep for right now,

00:52:31.380 --> 00:52:33.990
at least in the context of
this already somewhat detailed

00:52:33.990 --> 00:52:35.940
discussion of the effects of nicotine.

00:52:35.940 --> 00:52:38.400
But if we were to zoom out and just think

00:52:38.400 --> 00:52:39.810
about the effects of nicotine,

00:52:39.810 --> 00:52:41.523
we now have a very clear picture.

00:52:43.050 --> 00:52:44.550
Reward pathways are turned on.

00:52:44.550 --> 00:52:47.610
Attention is turned on.
Alertness is turned on.

00:52:47.610 --> 00:52:49.980
You feel better than you
felt a few minutes ago.

00:52:49.980 --> 00:52:54.600
Your blood pressure is
up. Your heart rate is up.

00:52:54.600 --> 00:52:59.490
Your preparedness for
thinking is elevated as well.

00:52:59.490 --> 00:53:02.610
And yet your body is somewhat relaxed.

00:53:02.610 --> 00:53:06.060
That's a very interesting
state of mind and body.

00:53:06.060 --> 00:53:11.060
Interesting because it's somewhat
ideal for cognitive work.

00:53:11.400 --> 00:53:13.800
Like if you were going to sit
down and work on a book or you're

00:53:13.800 --> 00:53:16.350
going to sit down and try and
figure out a hard math problem,

00:53:16.350 --> 00:53:17.910
or you're going to write a
letter that's been really

00:53:17.910 --> 00:53:19.140
challenging for you to write,

00:53:19.140 --> 00:53:21.750
or maybe that you're
really excited to write,

00:53:21.750 --> 00:53:22.770
but that you've been

00:53:22.770 --> 00:53:24.870
slow to get out the door
for whatever reason.

00:53:24.870 --> 00:53:27.540
I'm talking about my own
habits of procrastination.

00:53:27.540 --> 00:53:29.520
Well that state of being very alert,

00:53:29.520 --> 00:53:32.400
but your body being relaxed is almost,

00:53:32.400 --> 00:53:36.330
if not the optimal state for
getting mental work done,

00:53:36.330 --> 00:53:38.280
because if you're feeling
agitated in your body

00:53:38.280 --> 00:53:39.480
and you want to physically move your body,

00:53:39.480 --> 00:53:40.800
it's very hard to do cognitive work.

00:53:40.800 --> 00:53:43.260
At least the sorts of cognitive
work that involve typing

00:53:43.260 --> 00:53:45.240
or writing or these sorts of things.

00:53:45.240 --> 00:53:48.001
It's also the exact opposite
of the optimal state

00:53:48.001 --> 00:53:49.680
for physical performance,

00:53:49.680 --> 00:53:54.375
which is one of yes, also alertness,

00:53:54.375 --> 00:53:55.740
yes, also motivation and elevated mood.

00:53:55.740 --> 00:53:58.740
That's all wonderful
stuff to have in mind,

00:53:58.740 --> 00:54:01.380
literally when you are
exercising or competing

00:54:01.380 --> 00:54:03.480
in sport or something of that sort.

00:54:03.480 --> 00:54:04.650
But under those conditions,

00:54:04.650 --> 00:54:08.190
you really also want to
have a fast reaction time,

00:54:08.190 --> 00:54:10.500
a low latency for muscle
activation so that you can make

00:54:10.500 --> 00:54:12.960
coordinated muscle movements
in the ways that you need to,

00:54:12.960 --> 00:54:15.720
which is of course what's
required of physical endeavors.

00:54:15.720 --> 00:54:17.010
That tells us a few things, first of all,

00:54:17.010 --> 00:54:20.220
it tells us that nicotine is
going to be generally a bad

00:54:20.220 --> 00:54:24.930
idea for a pre-workout tool

00:54:24.930 --> 00:54:28.380
or for enhancing physical performance,

00:54:28.380 --> 00:54:30.510
however, it's apt to be,

00:54:30.510 --> 00:54:32.490
and in fact is an excellent tool

00:54:32.490 --> 00:54:34.440
for enhancing cognitive ability.

00:54:34.440 --> 00:54:35.273
And of course,

00:54:35.273 --> 00:54:37.830
that triggers my mind to
return to the anecdote about

00:54:37.830 --> 00:54:41.070
my Nobel prize winning
colleague who ingests nicotine

00:54:41.070 --> 00:54:43.350
by way of nicotine containing gum in order

00:54:43.350 --> 00:54:45.450
to increase levels of cognitive focus.

00:54:45.450 --> 00:54:47.550
Certainly not for going
out and playing sport.

00:54:47.550 --> 00:54:51.000
In fact, despite the fact
that he is very, very tall,

00:54:51.000 --> 00:54:52.360
he often points to himself

00:54:54.557 --> 00:54:56.257
in an appropriately funny way that

00:54:57.561 --> 00:54:58.770
despite being on the basketball
team of his high school,

00:54:58.770 --> 00:55:00.990
he is probably the worst
player that ever existed.

00:55:00.990 --> 00:55:02.850
And they only positioned him
there because of his height.

00:55:02.850 --> 00:55:06.090
And I guess his head was
designed to prevent balls

00:55:06.090 --> 00:55:07.350
from entering the basket.

00:55:07.350 --> 00:55:08.193
In any event,

00:55:09.030 --> 00:55:11.010
nicotine does seem to be very good

00:55:11.010 --> 00:55:14.490
at enhancing cognitive function,
at least in the short term,

00:55:14.490 --> 00:55:16.740
which is not to say that it
isn't without its side effects,

00:55:16.740 --> 00:55:17.970
which we will talk about.

00:55:17.970 --> 00:55:18.803
And again,

00:55:18.803 --> 00:55:20.270
those are side effects that
are independent of smoking

00:55:20.270 --> 00:55:23.520
or vaping or other forms
of ingesting nicotine.

00:55:23.520 --> 00:55:24.780
For instance,

00:55:24.780 --> 00:55:29.580
dipping or chewing tobacco
is known to cause a 50 fold,

00:55:29.580 --> 00:55:33.150
yes, 50 fold increase in mouth cancers,

00:55:33.150 --> 00:55:36.360
things like leukoplakia and just generally

00:55:36.360 --> 00:55:37.470
is terrible for your health.

00:55:37.470 --> 00:55:38.490
I'm sorry to break it to you,

00:55:38.490 --> 00:55:41.310
but if you're dipping
or you're using snuff

00:55:41.310 --> 00:55:42.510
or things of that sort,

00:55:42.510 --> 00:55:44.370
certainly I'm not going
to tell people what to do.

00:55:44.370 --> 00:55:46.353
That's not my role in life,

00:55:47.326 --> 00:55:50.580
but you are dramatically
increasing the probability of an

00:55:50.580 --> 00:55:54.150
oral cancer or of a mucosal
lining cancer of some sort.

00:55:54.150 --> 00:55:55.197
So it's not just that smoking

00:55:55.197 --> 00:55:56.910
and vaping are bad for your health.

00:55:56.910 --> 00:55:58.710
These other forms of delivery for nicotine

00:55:58.710 --> 00:55:59.907
can be bad for your health as well.

00:55:59.907 --> 00:56:02.640
Now whether or not ingesting
nicotine by way of nicotine

00:56:02.640 --> 00:56:06.150
containing gum or patch or
toothpick or other method

00:56:06.150 --> 00:56:07.860
is dangerous,

00:56:07.860 --> 00:56:09.720
for other reasons is a discussion

00:56:09.720 --> 00:56:11.220
that's important, right now,

00:56:11.220 --> 00:56:16.220
it appears that provided the
dosages are kept reasonable.

00:56:16.770 --> 00:56:18.870
We'll talk about what
reasonable means a little later,

00:56:18.870 --> 00:56:21.660
and the frequency is kept relatively low.

00:56:21.660 --> 00:56:24.390
So not relying on these things constantly.

00:56:24.390 --> 00:56:25.223
There may in fact

00:56:25.223 --> 00:56:27.750
be some benefit to ingesting
nicotine from time to time,

00:56:27.750 --> 00:56:32.400
provided that you are not
still developing your brain.

00:56:32.400 --> 00:56:33.420
Now in reality,

00:56:33.420 --> 00:56:36.000
neuroplasticity goes on
throughout the lifetime.

00:56:36.000 --> 00:56:37.920
Your life is actually one
long developmental arc.

00:56:37.920 --> 00:56:40.290
It's not like development
occurs and then stops,

00:56:40.290 --> 00:56:43.200
but certainly for people
before puberty, during puberty,

00:56:43.200 --> 00:56:47.220
and probably for the next 15
to 20 years after puberty,

00:56:47.220 --> 00:56:49.830
avoiding nicotine is probably a good idea.

00:56:49.830 --> 00:56:52.260
Now of course, development
is your entire life.

00:56:52.260 --> 00:56:53.820
It's not like development
starts and then ends,

00:56:53.820 --> 00:56:58.140
but certainly for people that
are 25 years old or younger,

00:56:58.140 --> 00:57:00.840
ingesting nicotine as a way
to enhance cognitive function

00:57:00.840 --> 00:57:02.430
is probably not the best idea.

00:57:02.430 --> 00:57:03.990
And certainly, please,

00:57:03.990 --> 00:57:07.410
for those of you that are
15 years old or younger,

00:57:07.410 --> 00:57:09.150
ingesting nicotine in any form,

00:57:09.150 --> 00:57:10.590
unless it's prescribed by your doctor

00:57:10.590 --> 00:57:13.200
for a very specific clinical reason,

00:57:13.200 --> 00:57:15.300
to me seems just like a terrible idea

00:57:15.300 --> 00:57:17.040
based on all the data that I've read.

00:57:17.040 --> 00:57:19.740
And the reason for that is
it's going to create a scenario

00:57:19.740 --> 00:57:22.320
of nicotine dependence in order
to achieve heightened levels

00:57:22.320 --> 00:57:25.260
of mood and alertness, et
cetera, and that's bad.

00:57:25.260 --> 00:57:26.420
And what we're effectively talking about

00:57:26.420 --> 00:57:28.470
is an addiction for nicotine directly,

00:57:28.470 --> 00:57:30.750
not necessarily the delivery device method

00:57:30.750 --> 00:57:31.740
like smoking or vaping,

00:57:31.740 --> 00:57:33.840
although it could pull
that in as an addictive

00:57:33.840 --> 00:57:35.820
or habit forming behavior as well,

00:57:35.820 --> 00:57:37.830
but you want to let your
neural circuits develop

00:57:37.830 --> 00:57:39.930
to the point where again,

00:57:39.930 --> 00:57:42.808
unless there's a clinical
need for a prescribed drug

00:57:42.808 --> 00:57:44.700
from a licensed physician
or psychiatrist, et cetera,

00:57:44.700 --> 00:57:47.640
that you're not relying
on chemical enhancement

00:57:47.640 --> 00:57:49.098
of these circuits.

00:57:49.098 --> 00:57:53.910
For people who are 25
years or older, and again,

00:57:53.910 --> 00:57:56.700
that's not a strict cutoff,
but roughly 25 years or older,

00:57:56.700 --> 00:58:00.150
but for those of you that are
thinking about using nicotine

00:58:00.150 --> 00:58:03.060
to enhance cognitive function as adults

00:58:03.060 --> 00:58:06.060
and your brain development
is slowing down, right.

00:58:06.060 --> 00:58:06.893
Never ceases,

00:58:06.893 --> 00:58:09.900
but is slowing down or has
slowed down to the point

00:58:09.900 --> 00:58:13.050
where we would say developmental
plasticity is largely over

00:58:13.050 --> 00:58:14.670
and you're now operating

00:58:14.670 --> 00:58:17.910
in the context of adult neuroplasticity.

00:58:17.910 --> 00:58:18.990
Well, in that case,

00:58:18.990 --> 00:58:21.670
there may be instances in which increasing

00:58:22.590 --> 00:58:24.060
acetylcholine, dopamine, et cetera,

00:58:24.060 --> 00:58:26.970
by way of nicotine ingestion
might be a good idea,

00:58:26.970 --> 00:58:28.920
but certainly not by smoking, vaping,

00:58:28.920 --> 00:58:32.910
or by direct contact of tobacco
to the mucosal lining tissue

00:58:32.910 --> 00:58:35.700
of the mouth or nose, so
called dipping or snuffing.

00:58:35.700 --> 00:58:37.050
For the last 20 minutes or so,

00:58:37.050 --> 00:58:38.910
we've been talking about
the biology of nicotine

00:58:38.910 --> 00:58:40.830
specifically, how it impacts the brain,

00:58:40.830 --> 00:58:42.990
how it impacts the body,
why it feels so good,

00:58:42.990 --> 00:58:44.490
why it can enhance focus.

00:58:44.490 --> 00:58:48.870
And we've largely set
aside smoking, vaping,

00:58:48.870 --> 00:58:51.030
dipping tobacco, and snuffing,

00:58:51.030 --> 00:58:53.640
and the negative effects
that those all have

00:58:53.640 --> 00:58:55.590
on mental and physical health.

00:58:55.590 --> 00:58:56.910
Working down from the top of the head

00:58:56.910 --> 00:59:01.020
to the bottom of the feet, we
can say that smoking, vaping,

00:59:01.020 --> 00:59:04.800
dipping, and snuffing negatively
impact every organ and

00:59:04.800 --> 00:59:08.220
tissue system and cell of the
body by virtue of the fact

00:59:08.220 --> 00:59:11.400
that they all damage
the endothelial cells.

00:59:11.400 --> 00:59:13.140
Again, the endothelial cells are the cells

00:59:13.140 --> 00:59:14.700
that make up the vasculature,

00:59:14.700 --> 00:59:18.150
which delivers blood and other nutrients

00:59:18.150 --> 00:59:21.060
to all the cells and organs
and tissues of the body.

00:59:21.060 --> 00:59:24.600
And those endothelial cells
are strongly and negatively

00:59:24.600 --> 00:59:27.693
impacted by all of the
practices that I just described.

00:59:28.557 --> 00:59:29.390
Now the way that that happens

00:59:29.390 --> 00:59:32.670
varies a little bit from each
one to the next, for instance,

00:59:32.670 --> 00:59:35.160
it has been estimated
that cigarettes contain

00:59:35.160 --> 00:59:38.430
anywhere from 4,000 to 7,000 toxins.

00:59:38.430 --> 00:59:40.440
Now the word toxins is a
real buzzword these days,

00:59:40.440 --> 00:59:44.550
you hear about detoxes and
toxins, but more specifically,

00:59:44.550 --> 00:59:46.920
we know that it contains carcinogens.

00:59:46.920 --> 00:59:49.410
These are cancer promoting compounds.

00:59:49.410 --> 00:59:50.460
For instance,

00:59:50.460 --> 00:59:53.790
we know that the tar in cigarettes,
even low tar cigarettes,

00:59:53.790 --> 00:59:55.740
as well as the ammonia within cigarettes,

00:59:55.740 --> 00:59:58.830
as well as the formaldehyde
contained within cigarettes,

00:59:58.830 --> 01:00:00.870
as well as the carbon
dioxide that's generated

01:00:00.870 --> 01:00:04.020
from smoking those cigarettes
are all carcinogens.

01:00:04.020 --> 01:00:07.290
Carbon dioxide also has the
negative effect of depleting

01:00:07.290 --> 01:00:09.600
the amount of oxygen that's
delivered to any and all

01:00:09.600 --> 01:00:12.540
of our tissues by way of the impact of

01:00:12.540 --> 01:00:16.680
carbon dioxide binding hemoglobin
and preventing hemoglobin

01:00:16.680 --> 01:00:19.470
from delivering oxygen to
the tissues of the body.

01:00:19.470 --> 01:00:23.490
So while there may be 4,000
or 4,500 or 7,000 toxins,

01:00:23.490 --> 01:00:24.660
depending on which cigarette,

01:00:24.660 --> 01:00:26.550
which papers they happen to be rolled in,

01:00:26.550 --> 01:00:28.380
whether or not they're
filtered or non-filtered,

01:00:28.380 --> 01:00:30.933
the type of tobacco, et cetera, et cetera,

01:00:31.830 --> 01:00:33.480
there are a tremendous number of toxins.

01:00:33.480 --> 01:00:35.850
And there are some very potent carcinogens

01:00:35.850 --> 01:00:37.890
within that long list of toxins.

01:00:37.890 --> 01:00:40.290
Again, ammonia, tar, formaldehyde,

01:00:40.290 --> 01:00:42.360
and carbon dioxide being the most potent

01:00:42.360 --> 01:00:43.710
of those carcinogens.

01:00:43.710 --> 01:00:45.690
Now the fact that there are
carcinogens in cigarettes

01:00:45.690 --> 01:00:48.270
sometimes lead cigarette
smokers and particularly the

01:00:48.270 --> 01:00:50.490
cigarette smokers that have
the hardest time quitting or

01:00:50.490 --> 01:00:53.010
that enjoy their cigarettes
the most from saying, well,

01:00:53.010 --> 01:00:55.140
listen, everything is a carcinogen
or everything kills you.

01:00:55.140 --> 01:00:58.800
Well certainly that's
not a true statement.

01:00:58.800 --> 01:01:01.500
And while there are other
carcinogens in the environment,

01:01:01.500 --> 01:01:04.380
so it environmental hazards like solvents.

01:01:04.380 --> 01:01:07.140
And even if you work in a
laboratory, for instance,

01:01:07.140 --> 01:01:09.900
we use in the laboratory,
DNA intercalating dyes,

01:01:09.900 --> 01:01:12.270
these are literally dyes
that allow us to see the DNA

01:01:12.270 --> 01:01:14.070
structure of cells and see the proteins

01:01:14.070 --> 01:01:16.200
they make and see the RNAs they make.

01:01:16.200 --> 01:01:17.520
And it's very important to wear gloves

01:01:17.520 --> 01:01:18.420
when you work with those things,

01:01:18.420 --> 01:01:21.229
because as the name
suggests, they intercalate,

01:01:21.229 --> 01:01:22.590
they actually get in
between the strands of DNA

01:01:22.590 --> 01:01:23.700
and separate them.

01:01:23.700 --> 01:01:26.220
They are mutagens, they mutate DNA.

01:01:26.220 --> 01:01:27.870
They are often carcinogens as well.

01:01:27.870 --> 01:01:29.160
So we have them in our laboratory,

01:01:29.160 --> 01:01:31.710
but we take certain precautions
to not have them negatively

01:01:31.710 --> 01:01:34.143
impact our health, safety
protocols and so on.

01:01:35.130 --> 01:01:38.160
We hear that there are
carcinogens in car exhaust

01:01:38.160 --> 01:01:40.290
and bus exhaust and in all sorts of things

01:01:40.290 --> 01:01:42.300
like pesticides and that's all true.

01:01:42.300 --> 01:01:44.550
So in the argument of probabilities,

01:01:44.550 --> 01:01:45.480
one would say, well,

01:01:45.480 --> 01:01:48.090
if there are all these other
carcinogens in the environment,

01:01:48.090 --> 01:01:51.390
why would you compound
their carcinogenic effect

01:01:51.390 --> 01:01:54.510
by smoking or vaping
or dipping or snuffing?

01:01:54.510 --> 01:01:56.640
But that usually doesn't get
people to quit smoking or doing

01:01:56.640 --> 01:01:58.500
those things because of
the powerful reinforcing

01:01:58.500 --> 01:02:00.180
effects of nicotine itself.

01:02:00.180 --> 01:02:01.013
So again,

01:02:01.013 --> 01:02:03.300
nicotine is the reinforcing element by way

01:02:03.300 --> 01:02:06.030
of triggering that dopamine
reinforcement pathway,

01:02:06.030 --> 01:02:07.740
the mesolimbic reward pathway.

01:02:07.740 --> 01:02:11.400
And of course there are all
the other additional effects

01:02:11.400 --> 01:02:13.110
of increased focus,

01:02:13.110 --> 01:02:15.210
such as increased ability to pay attention

01:02:15.210 --> 01:02:17.337
to work or to others that
lead to other rewards.

01:02:17.337 --> 01:02:20.010
And so then it becomes a
situation of compounding rewards.

01:02:20.010 --> 01:02:21.510
So it's not really about the cigarette,

01:02:21.510 --> 01:02:22.343
it's about the nicotine,

01:02:22.343 --> 01:02:23.610
and it's not really about the nicotine,

01:02:23.610 --> 01:02:25.620
it's about the dopamine
that the nicotine evokes.

01:02:25.620 --> 01:02:27.210
And then it's not really
about the dopamine

01:02:27.210 --> 01:02:30.030
that the nicotine evokes directly per se,

01:02:30.030 --> 01:02:31.950
but also about all the
positive effects that

01:02:31.950 --> 01:02:34.530
that increased dopamine results in.

01:02:34.530 --> 01:02:37.650
So we can easily circle back
to the negative effects of

01:02:37.650 --> 01:02:40.230
smoking, vaping, dipping, and snuffing,

01:02:40.230 --> 01:02:43.560
and say the endothelial
cells are disrupted.

01:02:43.560 --> 01:02:46.110
The endothelial cells are
involved in delivery of blood

01:02:46.110 --> 01:02:47.430
and other nutrients to every tissue

01:02:47.430 --> 01:02:51.060
of the body and smoking
vaping, dipping, and snuffing

01:02:51.060 --> 01:02:53.580
contain carcinogens, which
are cancer promoting.

01:02:53.580 --> 01:02:55.800
And because the epidemiological
studies are out there,

01:02:55.800 --> 01:02:58.950
we can actually arrive at some
very clear numbers as to how

01:02:58.950 --> 01:03:03.840
much life one will lose
from ingesting nicotine

01:03:03.840 --> 01:03:06.210
by way of those four delivery devices,

01:03:06.210 --> 01:03:09.420
or I should say any one of
those four delivery devices.

01:03:09.420 --> 01:03:11.430
Although I should also mention
that many people who are

01:03:11.430 --> 01:03:13.320
vaping are also smoking cigarettes

01:03:13.320 --> 01:03:14.760
is becoming increasingly common.

01:03:14.760 --> 01:03:16.860
A lot of people are using
vaping in one context

01:03:16.860 --> 01:03:18.030
and cigarettes in another,

01:03:18.030 --> 01:03:20.100
dipping in one context, vaping in another.

01:03:20.100 --> 01:03:23.190
But even for those that
only smoke or only vape

01:03:23.190 --> 01:03:25.470
or only dip or only snuff,

01:03:25.470 --> 01:03:30.060
the negative effects are
dramatic and calculable.

01:03:30.060 --> 01:03:32.010
So it is thought that for every pack

01:03:32.010 --> 01:03:34.560
of cigarettes consumed per day,

01:03:34.560 --> 01:03:37.080
so you could average that
out if you're a two a day

01:03:37.080 --> 01:03:40.440
cigarette smoker or a pack
a day cigarette smoker,

01:03:40.440 --> 01:03:41.520
two pack a day.

01:03:41.520 --> 01:03:44.793
For every pack of
cigarettes smoked per day,

01:03:45.750 --> 01:03:50.750
we can reliably estimate a 14
year reduction in lifespan.

01:03:51.450 --> 01:03:55.170
So cigarettes are literally
peeling years off your life.

01:03:55.170 --> 01:03:58.080
Now because of the way that the
brain works and the way that

01:03:58.080 --> 01:04:02.400
human beings anticipate the
future and can be grounded both

01:04:02.400 --> 01:04:04.950
in the present and the future
or the present and the past,

01:04:04.950 --> 01:04:06.210
that's just how the mind works, right?

01:04:06.210 --> 01:04:07.560
That's why we can think about the future,

01:04:07.560 --> 01:04:10.530
but also realize where we
are in time and space today.

01:04:10.530 --> 01:04:12.840
Because of that, many
people will say, well,

01:04:12.840 --> 01:04:14.817
I enjoy cigarettes or I enjoy vaping.

01:04:14.817 --> 01:04:16.590
And so at least while I'm
here, I'm enjoying it.

01:04:16.590 --> 01:04:18.540
And that's because the dopamine system

01:04:18.540 --> 01:04:22.770
is not very good at
understanding opportunity costs.

01:04:22.770 --> 01:04:25.530
That is what we would be
doing in those 14 years

01:04:25.530 --> 01:04:27.450
and what we would be
enjoying and the enrichment

01:04:27.450 --> 01:04:30.720
that we would get if we were
to live into those 14 years.

01:04:30.720 --> 01:04:32.790
So at some level the smoker or vaper

01:04:32.790 --> 01:04:35.790
is being rational when they
say, yeah, but I enjoy this.

01:04:35.790 --> 01:04:37.950
And so the years lost,

01:04:37.950 --> 01:04:40.380
I can't really register that
because it's hard to register

01:04:40.380 --> 01:04:43.080
what you don't have and
what you've never had.

01:04:43.080 --> 01:04:44.610
On the other hand,

01:04:44.610 --> 01:04:47.640
we can also point to the
statistic that there is

01:04:47.640 --> 01:04:51.270
this 50 fold increase in
mouth cancers from dipping.

01:04:51.270 --> 01:04:53.430
And there are nasal cancers as well

01:04:53.430 --> 01:04:58.050
that are greatly increased
by snuffing and from smoking,

01:04:58.050 --> 01:05:02.040
and now we know based on
data from vaping that the

01:05:02.040 --> 01:05:05.580
endothelial cell damage
and the direct effects

01:05:05.580 --> 01:05:07.380
of damage to the lungs from tars,

01:05:07.380 --> 01:05:08.760
and even if people are vaping,

01:05:08.760 --> 01:05:11.970
which tends to have lower
tars than do cigarettes,

01:05:11.970 --> 01:05:13.650
even for people that are vaping,

01:05:13.650 --> 01:05:17.130
greatly increased probability of stroke,

01:05:17.130 --> 01:05:18.963
of peripheral vascular disease.

01:05:19.892 --> 01:05:22.051
So this is peripheral
pain because remember,

01:05:22.051 --> 01:05:22.980
blood is delivering, not just blood,

01:05:22.980 --> 01:05:25.110
but other nutrients and
it's clearing things

01:05:25.110 --> 01:05:26.340
out from tissues.

01:05:26.340 --> 01:05:29.460
So there's an accumulation of
literally toxins and debris

01:05:29.460 --> 01:05:31.710
that cells generate all
the time, which is healthy,

01:05:31.710 --> 01:05:34.140
but then the normally is cleared away

01:05:34.140 --> 01:05:35.340
by the endothelial cells

01:05:35.340 --> 01:05:38.160
and by other cell types
of the immune system,

01:05:38.160 --> 01:05:41.220
that's all increased in people that engage

01:05:41.220 --> 01:05:43.743
in these nicotine
delivery device behaviors.

01:05:44.970 --> 01:05:47.130
Rates of heart attack, rates of stroke,

01:05:47.130 --> 01:05:49.950
rates of cognitive
decline are all increased.

01:05:49.950 --> 01:05:51.060
Now you might say cognitive decline,

01:05:51.060 --> 01:05:54.720
I thought that nicotine increases
the likelihood that we can

01:05:54.720 --> 01:05:57.510
maintain healthy neural
function and cognitive capacity.

01:05:57.510 --> 01:05:59.313
It might even increase cognitive capacity

01:05:59.313 --> 01:06:01.920
in a potent way in the short
term, and indeed it does.

01:06:01.920 --> 01:06:03.390
However,

01:06:03.390 --> 01:06:06.210
cigarette smoking and vaping are now known

01:06:06.210 --> 01:06:10.140
to dramatically decrease
cognitive function

01:06:10.140 --> 01:06:12.360
because one of the key
things about the brain

01:06:12.360 --> 01:06:15.090
is that it is the most
metabolically demanding organ,

01:06:15.090 --> 01:06:16.680
which means it consumes a lot of glucose.

01:06:16.680 --> 01:06:18.840
Or even if you're
ketogenic, you need ketones,

01:06:18.840 --> 01:06:21.150
you need nutrients getting
to the neurons in other cell

01:06:21.150 --> 01:06:22.800
types of the brain and nervous system

01:06:22.800 --> 01:06:24.570
in order for it to function properly.

01:06:24.570 --> 01:06:27.480
And when you disrupt a vasculature
through this endothelial

01:06:27.480 --> 01:06:30.240
cell dysfunction, you get
things like interstitial,

01:06:30.240 --> 01:06:32.460
which just means in the
spaces between dysfunction.

01:06:32.460 --> 01:06:34.170
So it's not just beating up

01:06:34.170 --> 01:06:35.610
the endothelial cells themselves,

01:06:35.610 --> 01:06:38.160
but the spaces between the
cells is being disrupted.

01:06:38.160 --> 01:06:39.930
There's a lot of debris
that accumulates there.

01:06:39.930 --> 01:06:41.550
And as a consequence,

01:06:41.550 --> 01:06:44.400
the brain just simply
will not function as well.

01:06:44.400 --> 01:06:46.320
So you start getting
short term memory lapses,

01:06:46.320 --> 01:06:47.820
you start getting working memory lapses,

01:06:47.820 --> 01:06:49.320
working memory is the sort of memory

01:06:49.320 --> 01:06:52.560
if someone tells you their
seven digit phone number,

01:06:52.560 --> 01:06:54.480
typically nowadays people
just share their info,

01:06:54.480 --> 01:06:56.280
but seven digit phone number or a sequence

01:06:56.280 --> 01:06:57.330
of numbers or an address

01:06:57.330 --> 01:06:58.740
and your inability to remember that.

01:06:58.740 --> 01:07:00.570
So you're walking back to the
kitchen sink and you can't

01:07:00.570 --> 01:07:02.490
remember what you were trying to remember

01:07:02.490 --> 01:07:04.560
just a short moment ago,
that's working memory.

01:07:04.560 --> 01:07:07.410
Working memory suffers, long term memory,

01:07:07.410 --> 01:07:10.530
projective or intergrade
memories into the future.

01:07:10.530 --> 01:07:13.590
How can you remember things in the future

01:07:13.590 --> 01:07:14.460
that haven't occurred yet?

01:07:14.460 --> 01:07:18.660
Well this is more of a memory
for future plans or ideas

01:07:18.660 --> 01:07:20.730
and planning for things that are to come.

01:07:20.730 --> 01:07:23.280
So what we can very reliably
say is that currently

01:07:23.280 --> 01:07:25.800
more than 1 billion, billion,

01:07:25.800 --> 01:07:29.250
more than 1 billion people consume tobacco

01:07:29.250 --> 01:07:30.990
in order to get their nicotine

01:07:30.990 --> 01:07:33.180
because that's really
the reinforcing element

01:07:33.180 --> 01:07:34.113
within tobacco,

01:07:35.130 --> 01:07:37.830
more than 1 billion people
consume tobacco in the form

01:07:37.830 --> 01:07:41.040
of cigarettes every day.

01:07:41.040 --> 01:07:42.750
A growing number of people,

01:07:42.750 --> 01:07:46.950
more than half a billion people
now are starting to vape.

01:07:46.950 --> 01:07:50.550
The estimates range from
200 million to 500 million.

01:07:50.550 --> 01:07:52.290
And there's a lot of
debate about this because

01:07:52.290 --> 01:07:54.960
a lot of people are
hybrid smokers and vapers,

01:07:54.960 --> 01:07:57.030
meaning they do one or both

01:07:57.030 --> 01:07:58.560
depending on time of day and location,

01:07:58.560 --> 01:07:59.910
as I mentioned before,

01:07:59.910 --> 01:08:04.170
but now you start to see how
you can get to the number

01:08:04.170 --> 01:08:06.540
that billions of people
are consuming tobacco.

01:08:06.540 --> 01:08:08.280
Because of course you
also have your people

01:08:08.280 --> 01:08:10.410
that are dipping and your
people that are snuffing.

01:08:10.410 --> 01:08:11.340
And as I mentioned before,

01:08:11.340 --> 01:08:13.860
you have people that
are engaging in multiple

01:08:13.860 --> 01:08:15.570
of these behaviors.

01:08:15.570 --> 01:08:18.600
So billions of people on planet Earth,

01:08:18.600 --> 01:08:22.530
anywhere from one eighth to
a quarter of human beings,

01:08:22.530 --> 01:08:24.000
incredible right?

01:08:24.000 --> 01:08:25.620
Anywhere from one eighth to a quarter of

01:08:25.620 --> 01:08:28.860
human beings are consuming
tobacco in one form or another

01:08:28.860 --> 01:08:32.790
in order to get their
nicotine one way or another.

01:08:32.790 --> 01:08:34.410
And as a consequence,

01:08:34.410 --> 01:08:36.068
are peeling years off their life,

01:08:36.068 --> 01:08:39.330
dramatically increasing
the probability of cancer,

01:08:39.330 --> 01:08:41.610
stroke, heart attack,

01:08:41.610 --> 01:08:45.390
peripheral neuropathies,
brain dysfunction,

01:08:45.390 --> 01:08:48.180
meaning cognitive dysfunction,
memory impairment,

01:08:48.180 --> 01:08:49.890
sexual dysfunction.

01:08:49.890 --> 01:08:51.069
There are a number of studies

01:08:51.069 --> 01:08:52.710
that have looked at increases in cortisol.

01:08:52.710 --> 01:08:55.710
And while minor, those increases do exist.

01:08:55.710 --> 01:08:57.810
Decreases in growth
hormone, and while minor,

01:08:57.810 --> 01:09:00.330
those decreases exist,

01:09:00.330 --> 01:09:05.070
but even setting aside the
negative impact on endocrine,

01:09:05.070 --> 01:09:06.810
on hormone factors,

01:09:06.810 --> 01:09:10.233
it's very, very clear
that smoking, vaping,

01:09:11.190 --> 01:09:14.640
dipping, and snuffing are
among the worst things

01:09:14.640 --> 01:09:16.500
that we can do for our health.

01:09:16.500 --> 01:09:19.770
They are among the leading
causes of preventable death

01:09:19.770 --> 01:09:21.903
and debilitating life conditions,

01:09:23.310 --> 01:09:25.140
which may lead you to the question as

01:09:25.140 --> 01:09:29.040
to why in the world would people do this?

01:09:29.040 --> 01:09:33.030
Well it turns out most
of them don't want to.

01:09:33.030 --> 01:09:35.470
In the best surveys that one can find of

01:09:36.665 --> 01:09:37.590
if you could quit smoking,

01:09:37.590 --> 01:09:39.990
if you could quit vaping, would you,

01:09:39.990 --> 01:09:43.260
what you find is that at
least for cigarette smokers,

01:09:43.260 --> 01:09:47.580
70% want to quit.

01:09:47.580 --> 01:09:49.230
They would love to quit.

01:09:49.230 --> 01:09:51.990
And yet they find that to
be exceedingly difficult.

01:09:51.990 --> 01:09:54.720
And the reason they find it
to be exceedingly difficult is

01:09:54.720 --> 01:09:56.640
because of all the brain neurochemistry

01:09:56.640 --> 01:09:57.570
that we talked about before.

01:09:57.570 --> 01:10:00.480
The reason I spent close
to 20 minutes talking about

01:10:00.480 --> 01:10:02.010
dopamine and the mesolimbic pathway,

01:10:02.010 --> 01:10:06.570
acetylcholine and nucleus
basalis, and epinephrine,

01:10:06.570 --> 01:10:08.880
and the relaxation of muscles
in the periphery and the

01:10:08.880 --> 01:10:12.420
increase in readiness
in the body and brain is

01:10:12.420 --> 01:10:14.880
that all of those combine
to make nicotine one

01:10:14.880 --> 01:10:17.670
of the most powerful and
potent cognitive enhancers

01:10:17.670 --> 01:10:19.830
and to some extent physical enhancer,

01:10:19.830 --> 01:10:21.570
although, as I mentioned before,

01:10:21.570 --> 01:10:25.590
the total number of physical
exercise or physical

01:10:25.590 --> 01:10:30.030
sport promoting effects of
nicotine is very, very small,

01:10:30.030 --> 01:10:31.770
if not zero.

01:10:31.770 --> 01:10:34.920
There are certain conditions
under which one might imagine

01:10:34.920 --> 01:10:39.210
using nicotine specifically
for cognitive enhancement where

01:10:39.210 --> 01:10:42.510
performance of complex motor
skills would sort of outweigh

01:10:42.510 --> 01:10:45.090
the negative effects on
the neuromuscular system,

01:10:45.090 --> 01:10:47.760
our ability to generate
coordinated movements.

01:10:47.760 --> 01:10:50.100
There was actually an excellent
study looking at the effects

01:10:50.100 --> 01:10:52.620
of nicotine, not by smoking delivery,

01:10:52.620 --> 01:10:56.460
but by different delivery
mechanism in which they looked at

01:10:56.460 --> 01:10:59.490
performance of hitters in baseball.

01:10:59.490 --> 01:11:01.230
The experiment was kind
of an interesting one,

01:11:01.230 --> 01:11:03.390
even though these were fairly
skilled baseball players,

01:11:03.390 --> 01:11:05.880
what they had them do
was hit a ball off a tee,

01:11:05.880 --> 01:11:08.670
as I recall growing up and I admittedly

01:11:08.670 --> 01:11:09.690
I didn't play much baseball,

01:11:09.690 --> 01:11:11.670
but you start off on tee-ball and then

01:11:11.670 --> 01:11:13.320
you would go, I think it
was to pitching machine.

01:11:13.320 --> 01:11:15.017
Then they would use actual
pitchers who throw the ball.

01:11:15.017 --> 01:11:17.490
But in this case it was a couple

01:11:17.490 --> 01:11:19.230
of different baseball related tasks.

01:11:19.230 --> 01:11:21.810
They'd have people hit the ball off a tee,

01:11:21.810 --> 01:11:23.550
but they had to direct that hit

01:11:23.550 --> 01:11:25.830
toward a ball atop another tee.

01:11:25.830 --> 01:11:27.660
So it's fairly precise targeting that's

01:11:27.660 --> 01:11:30.060
required in order to knock
that ball off the other tee.

01:11:30.060 --> 01:11:32.700
So this is an activity that
involves not just motor output,

01:11:32.700 --> 01:11:34.470
but coordinated motor output,

01:11:34.470 --> 01:11:36.720
and not just coordinated motor output,

01:11:36.720 --> 01:11:39.360
but directed, coordinated output

01:11:39.360 --> 01:11:42.540
that requires some, as we
would say, top down processing.

01:11:42.540 --> 01:11:44.850
This isn't the kind of thing
that can be done reflexively.

01:11:44.850 --> 01:11:47.340
This is the kind of thing
where the forebrain,

01:11:47.340 --> 01:11:49.350
the prefrontal cortex in
particular has to be heavily

01:11:49.350 --> 01:11:51.690
involved in order to
suppress certain actions

01:11:51.690 --> 01:11:53.250
and then create other actions.

01:11:53.250 --> 01:11:55.530
So the basic takeaway of
the study is that nicotine

01:11:55.530 --> 01:11:58.020
delivered by way of nicotine gum,

01:11:58.020 --> 01:12:01.950
not by smoking, dipping,
or snuffing was able

01:12:01.950 --> 01:12:04.320
to increase cognitive
performance and motor output.

01:12:04.320 --> 01:12:09.180
So a rare circumstance where
a specific set of demands that

01:12:09.180 --> 01:12:12.900
involved both cognitive
engagement and physical engagement

01:12:12.900 --> 01:12:15.600
showed a slight but
significant improvement.

01:12:15.600 --> 01:12:17.670
But again, in most cases,

01:12:17.670 --> 01:12:21.300
nicotine is just simply not
going to improve physical output

01:12:21.300 --> 01:12:24.960
if it's delivered through
a smoked cigarette,

01:12:24.960 --> 01:12:27.810
through vaping, through
dipping, or through snuffing.

01:12:27.810 --> 01:12:30.780
So if all these behaviors
are terrible for essentially

01:12:30.780 --> 01:12:32.700
every aspect of our
health, frankly, I mean,

01:12:32.700 --> 01:12:33.533
when you look at the literature,

01:12:33.533 --> 01:12:35.700
it's terrible for pregnant
women, it's terrible for kids,

01:12:35.700 --> 01:12:37.380
it's terrible for older people,

01:12:37.380 --> 01:12:38.910
it's terrible for younger people.

01:12:38.910 --> 01:12:42.960
You really cannot find a scenario
in which smoking, vaping,

01:12:42.960 --> 01:12:44.940
dipping, or snuffing are good for us.

01:12:44.940 --> 01:12:48.390
And yet people like the effects
on the brain and they feel

01:12:48.390 --> 01:12:50.970
quite addicted to them, even
if they say they're not,

01:12:50.970 --> 01:12:54.390
most of those people would
be unwilling to give up their

01:12:54.390 --> 01:12:58.140
practice of nicotine delivery
for more than a few hours.

01:12:58.140 --> 01:12:59.514
In fact,

01:12:59.514 --> 01:13:00.690
if you look at the effects of
withdrawal and we are going

01:13:00.690 --> 01:13:03.423
to talk about what withdrawal
of nicotine looks like,

01:13:03.423 --> 01:13:07.590
what you find is that
as soon as four hours

01:13:07.590 --> 01:13:09.930
after the last ingestion of nicotine

01:13:09.930 --> 01:13:13.470
by way of cigarette or vaping
or dipping or snuffing,

01:13:13.470 --> 01:13:14.990
what people start to experience

01:13:14.990 --> 01:13:18.270
is some agitation, craving
for nicotine, of course.

01:13:18.270 --> 01:13:20.850
And while craving is
kind of a vague concept,

01:13:20.850 --> 01:13:23.400
it's actually a very specific
biological mechanism.

01:13:23.400 --> 01:13:26.880
It's the drop in dopamine
that's starting to occur

01:13:26.880 --> 01:13:30.030
so much so that there's a drop
in dopamine below baseline.

01:13:30.030 --> 01:13:32.580
That is the increase in
dopamine that would normally

01:13:32.580 --> 01:13:35.670
be experienced from
smoking, vaping, dipping,

01:13:35.670 --> 01:13:38.100
or snuffing is now not happening.

01:13:38.100 --> 01:13:40.590
And in fact, the levels of
dopamine are dropping below

01:13:40.590 --> 01:13:42.150
where they would've been even

01:13:42.150 --> 01:13:44.550
without performing that behavior.

01:13:44.550 --> 01:13:46.800
So that's what craving is.

01:13:46.800 --> 01:13:50.520
And withdrawal is an increased
sense of that craving as well

01:13:50.520 --> 01:13:54.030
as a lot of negative stuff
like stomach aches or nausea

01:13:54.030 --> 01:13:57.000
or irritability, and often
collections of all of those.

01:13:57.000 --> 01:13:58.920
So because these nicotine
delivery approaches

01:13:58.920 --> 01:14:01.140
are so terrible for our health,

01:14:01.140 --> 01:14:05.070
and also because as many as 70% of people

01:14:05.070 --> 01:14:07.050
who smoke would like to quit,

01:14:07.050 --> 01:14:09.270
but either feel they can't
because they've tried

01:14:09.270 --> 01:14:13.230
and failed repeatedly often,
or because it's just too scary,

01:14:13.230 --> 01:14:15.510
meaning the reinforcing
properties are too strong and

01:14:15.510 --> 01:14:18.300
therefore they can't
imagine living without them

01:14:18.300 --> 01:14:21.000
or the withdrawal effects are too strong

01:14:21.000 --> 01:14:23.760
and they can't imagine living with those.

01:14:23.760 --> 01:14:26.733
Well then is there hope
for quitting smoking,

01:14:26.733 --> 01:14:29.340
vaping, snuffing, or dipping?

01:14:29.340 --> 01:14:31.920
And the short answer, fortunately is yes,

01:14:31.920 --> 01:14:33.360
there are excellent ways to do that.

01:14:33.360 --> 01:14:36.930
And some of them are
single event treatments.

01:14:36.930 --> 01:14:39.540
And we're going to talk
about those shortly,

01:14:39.540 --> 01:14:40.380
but before we do that,

01:14:40.380 --> 01:14:42.480
I want to highlight one very brief point,

01:14:42.480 --> 01:14:46.620
which is that nicotine is
not the cause of cancer.

01:14:46.620 --> 01:14:48.720
Nicotine is not the carcinogen,

01:14:48.720 --> 01:14:53.430
it's the other things
in tobacco or associated

01:14:53.430 --> 01:14:57.390
with the nicotine delivery
device that are causing cancer.

01:14:57.390 --> 01:15:00.570
And I should mention the
other negative impacts

01:15:00.570 --> 01:15:01.654
on our health in particular

01:15:01.654 --> 01:15:06.090
by way of disrupted endothelial
blood vessel function.

01:15:06.090 --> 01:15:07.950
Now that leads us to this issue of vaping,

01:15:07.950 --> 01:15:09.630
because as many of you know,

01:15:09.630 --> 01:15:12.000
and probably are thinking
as I've been delivering

01:15:12.000 --> 01:15:15.933
this information, people
don't vape tobacco.

01:15:16.770 --> 01:15:19.980
The way that vaping pens
are designed is that

01:15:19.980 --> 01:15:22.800
it includes some liquid, it involves heat,

01:15:22.800 --> 01:15:25.650
and it does not involve
the burning off of tobacco.

01:15:25.650 --> 01:15:29.700
In fact, there's a constant
updating of the engineering

01:15:29.700 --> 01:15:33.020
of these vape pens so that
they can be very low heat.

01:15:33.020 --> 01:15:37.620
In some cases they use even
non heating approaches to

01:15:37.620 --> 01:15:39.690
vaporize the nicotine and allow it

01:15:39.690 --> 01:15:42.630
to enter the bloodstream very quickly.

01:15:42.630 --> 01:15:47.630
I must say in a lot of ways,
vaping resembles crack cocaine.

01:15:47.760 --> 01:15:51.180
The reason why vaping and
crack cocaine are so similar

01:15:51.180 --> 01:15:55.170
is the speed of entry of
nicotine into the bloodstream.

01:15:55.170 --> 01:15:57.420
This isn't an episode about cocaine,

01:15:57.420 --> 01:16:00.180
but I just want to very briefly
touch on some of the delivery

01:16:00.180 --> 01:16:02.580
routes for cocaine because they parallel

01:16:02.580 --> 01:16:05.160
a lot of the delivery routes for nicotine.

01:16:05.160 --> 01:16:06.900
And we can learn a lot about

01:16:06.900 --> 01:16:09.630
drug pharmacokinetics and dopamine

01:16:09.630 --> 01:16:13.263
if we look at the parallels
between cocaine and nicotine.

01:16:14.250 --> 01:16:18.360
I'll preface this by saying
cocaine is a terrible drug.

01:16:18.360 --> 01:16:21.180
It is actually a schedule two
drug in the United States,

01:16:21.180 --> 01:16:22.770
which means that it has a very,

01:16:22.770 --> 01:16:26.010
very limited yet still
present medical application,

01:16:26.010 --> 01:16:29.700
mainly as an anesthetic
in certain laboratory

01:16:29.700 --> 01:16:31.560
and hospital conditions.

01:16:31.560 --> 01:16:32.940
But aside from that,

01:16:32.940 --> 01:16:35.490
it's very clear that cocaine
is one of the most debilitating

01:16:35.490 --> 01:16:37.860
drugs that humans can
use because of the way

01:16:37.860 --> 01:16:39.630
that it impacts the dopamine system.

01:16:39.630 --> 01:16:43.170
And it basically creates a
loop whereby the only thing

01:16:43.170 --> 01:16:46.890
that can really trigger
dopamine release is cocaine.

01:16:46.890 --> 01:16:48.480
And as I've said before,

01:16:48.480 --> 01:16:51.210
the way that I define
addiction is it's a progressive

01:16:51.210 --> 01:16:53.370
narrowing of the things
that bring people pleasure.

01:16:53.370 --> 01:16:55.440
Cocaine certainly falls into the category

01:16:55.440 --> 01:16:58.770
of addictive drugs,
strongly addictive drugs.

01:16:58.770 --> 01:16:59.603
And in fact,

01:16:59.603 --> 01:17:01.560
it has the additionally pernicious feature

01:17:01.560 --> 01:17:04.890
that after using cocaine
for some period of time,

01:17:04.890 --> 01:17:07.500
the amount of dopamine that's
released becomes progressively

01:17:07.500 --> 01:17:10.440
lower and lower and lower so
that people can never get back

01:17:10.440 --> 01:17:13.890
to a state in which dopamine
release is ever as great

01:17:13.890 --> 01:17:15.630
as it was the first time they did cocaine

01:17:15.630 --> 01:17:17.345
or prior to doing cocaine.

01:17:17.345 --> 01:17:22.345
Now with a long period of
no cocaine use whatsoever

01:17:23.130 --> 01:17:25.860
and protecting the dopamine system

01:17:25.860 --> 01:17:27.420
in a number of different ways,

01:17:27.420 --> 01:17:30.420
people can often, not always but often

01:17:30.420 --> 01:17:32.730
recover their dopamine system,

01:17:32.730 --> 01:17:34.803
if not completely then near completely.

01:17:36.285 --> 01:17:39.030
So by all means, if you have
a problem with cocaine, quit,

01:17:39.030 --> 01:17:41.130
find a way to quit, get treatment,

01:17:41.130 --> 01:17:42.810
get over that one way or another.

01:17:42.810 --> 01:17:44.940
We have an episode with an expert guest,

01:17:44.940 --> 01:17:46.350
Dr. Anna Lembke,

01:17:46.350 --> 01:17:47.910
who's a physician and the chief

01:17:47.910 --> 01:17:49.590
of the Stanford School of Medicine

01:17:49.590 --> 01:17:51.810
dual diagnosis addiction clinic,

01:17:51.810 --> 01:17:54.810
a world expert in dopamine
and addictive substances

01:17:54.810 --> 01:17:56.220
and addictions of all kinds.

01:17:56.220 --> 01:17:58.724
You can find that episode
at hubermanlab.com.

01:17:58.724 --> 01:18:01.320
It's filled with information
about how to get over different

01:18:01.320 --> 01:18:03.150
types of addictions,
including cocaine addiction.

01:18:03.150 --> 01:18:05.370
Also check out her absolutely phenomenal

01:18:05.370 --> 01:18:07.260
and indeed important
book "Dopamine Nation",

01:18:07.260 --> 01:18:09.720
which touches on some of this as well.

01:18:09.720 --> 01:18:12.000
And in the show note
captions for the episode

01:18:12.000 --> 01:18:14.160
where Dr. Lembke was my guest,

01:18:14.160 --> 01:18:16.590
you can also find some additional
resources related to that.

01:18:16.590 --> 01:18:19.350
So cocaine is terrible. That
needs to be acknowledged.

01:18:19.350 --> 01:18:21.810
It should be avoided and you
should find a way to quit it

01:18:21.810 --> 01:18:23.283
if you are currently using.

01:18:24.420 --> 01:18:25.770
With that said,

01:18:25.770 --> 01:18:29.040
the delivery mechanism for
cocaine strongly parallels

01:18:29.040 --> 01:18:30.930
the delivery mechanisms for nicotine.

01:18:30.930 --> 01:18:32.640
That is people will snort cocaine,

01:18:32.640 --> 01:18:34.740
which is a lot like snuffing or dipping.

01:18:34.740 --> 01:18:36.300
That is when people snort cocaine,

01:18:36.300 --> 01:18:38.820
they're bringing cocaine into
proximity or into contact,

01:18:38.820 --> 01:18:41.880
really with the mucosal
lining of the nasal passages,

01:18:41.880 --> 01:18:44.670
which then allows the
psychoactive substances

01:18:44.670 --> 01:18:47.010
to permeate into the bloodstream.

01:18:47.010 --> 01:18:49.140
Very seldom do people eat it,

01:18:49.140 --> 01:18:52.140
although that does
happen from time to time,

01:18:52.140 --> 01:18:55.110
people will inject it,
as so-called mainlining,

01:18:55.110 --> 01:18:57.720
which is a very rapid entry
into the bloodstream because

01:18:57.720 --> 01:18:59.730
it's direct application to the bloodstream

01:18:59.730 --> 01:19:00.753
by way of injection.

01:19:01.590 --> 01:19:02.423
And then there's crack cocaine,

01:19:02.423 --> 01:19:05.610
which is essentially like
a vaporizing of the cocaine

01:19:05.610 --> 01:19:07.710
from a so-called cocaine rock,

01:19:07.710 --> 01:19:12.690
that somewhat resembles
vaping of nicotine.

01:19:12.690 --> 01:19:15.870
So while the vape pen
involves a liquid that's sold,

01:19:15.870 --> 01:19:17.760
a cartridge that contains nicotine

01:19:17.760 --> 01:19:21.843
and often other flavors as
well, flavorings I should say,

01:19:23.370 --> 01:19:27.240
both crack cocaine and
vaping cause very rapid

01:19:27.240 --> 01:19:29.130
increases in the relative substances

01:19:29.130 --> 01:19:30.810
that are psychoactive,
in the case of cocaine,

01:19:30.810 --> 01:19:33.510
that would be cocaine and
the increase in dopamine

01:19:33.510 --> 01:19:35.550
in the brain and body I should mention.

01:19:35.550 --> 01:19:37.080
And in the case of vaping,

01:19:37.080 --> 01:19:38.730
there's a very rapid increase

01:19:38.730 --> 01:19:40.320
in blood concentrations of nicotine,

01:19:40.320 --> 01:19:43.380
much faster than occur
with cigarette smoking

01:19:43.380 --> 01:19:45.840
or other modes of nicotine delivery.

01:19:45.840 --> 01:19:49.800
So that speed of onset turns
out to be a critical parameter

01:19:49.800 --> 01:19:53.790
because the speed of onset
of nicotine is going to also

01:19:53.790 --> 01:19:56.000
determine the speed of release of dopamine

01:19:56.000 --> 01:19:58.230
in the nucleus accumbens
and ventral tegmental area,

01:19:58.230 --> 01:20:00.570
that mesolimbic reward pathway.

01:20:00.570 --> 01:20:02.220
And with the mesolimbic reward pathway,

01:20:02.220 --> 01:20:04.470
it's not just about the peak,

01:20:04.470 --> 01:20:08.250
meaning the maximal amount
of dopamine that's released,

01:20:08.250 --> 01:20:11.070
but it's the time course,
how steep the curve is,

01:20:11.070 --> 01:20:12.720
how quickly that dopamine increases

01:20:12.720 --> 01:20:15.240
that's going to determine how reinforcing,

01:20:15.240 --> 01:20:17.580
how habit forming and indeed how addictive

01:20:17.580 --> 01:20:19.083
a particular substance is.

01:20:19.950 --> 01:20:22.470
One of the major important
differences between vaping and

01:20:22.470 --> 01:20:24.420
cigarette smoking is that the onset

01:20:24.420 --> 01:20:27.660
of dopamine release from vaping is faster.

01:20:27.660 --> 01:20:31.230
And even a subtle increase
in the rate at which dopamine

01:20:31.230 --> 01:20:36.060
increases in the mesolimbic
reward pathway can make a given

01:20:36.060 --> 01:20:40.800
amount of nicotine much more
habit forming and addictive.

01:20:40.800 --> 01:20:43.830
Now this probably explains
the fact that many,

01:20:43.830 --> 01:20:45.120
many more people, in particular,

01:20:45.120 --> 01:20:47.370
young people age 25 and younger

01:20:47.370 --> 01:20:50.340
are vaping at phenomenal rates.

01:20:50.340 --> 01:20:52.830
People are starting to vape
in just about everywhere.

01:20:52.830 --> 01:20:54.780
You see this in restaurants,
you see it on the street,

01:20:54.780 --> 01:20:57.270
indeed you even see it in the classroom.

01:20:57.270 --> 01:21:00.360
This has actually become
sort of sport of sorts.

01:21:00.360 --> 01:21:02.040
Certainly not something I encourage.

01:21:02.040 --> 01:21:03.990
In fact, when I learned about this from

01:21:05.465 --> 01:21:06.750
a new college student is telling me

01:21:06.750 --> 01:21:09.270
that many college students are
actually bringing vape pens

01:21:09.270 --> 01:21:10.103
into the classroom.

01:21:10.103 --> 01:21:11.880
I think this is also
happening in high schools

01:21:11.880 --> 01:21:13.860
and even junior high schools.

01:21:13.860 --> 01:21:16.560
This is a really concerning practice.

01:21:16.560 --> 01:21:18.810
And here I'm not trying to come across as

01:21:18.810 --> 01:21:20.100
the anti vaping crusader,

01:21:20.100 --> 01:21:22.560
but I must say given the negative effects

01:21:22.560 --> 01:21:24.300
on one's health in the long term,

01:21:24.300 --> 01:21:28.080
but also given these exceedingly
powerful reward properties

01:21:28.080 --> 01:21:30.810
of nicotine entering
the bloodstream quickly,

01:21:30.810 --> 01:21:33.360
and dopamine being released very quickly

01:21:33.360 --> 01:21:35.700
from the mesolimbic reward pathway,

01:21:35.700 --> 01:21:38.340
what we're seeing out
there makes total sense.

01:21:38.340 --> 01:21:42.030
Young people's brains are
essentially getting wired

01:21:42.030 --> 01:21:45.240
to the expectation that dopamine
is going to be increased

01:21:45.240 --> 01:21:48.240
dramatically and quickly
under their control,

01:21:48.240 --> 01:21:50.160
except it's under their control

01:21:50.160 --> 01:21:52.500
only in a very narrow
set of circumstances.

01:21:52.500 --> 01:21:54.360
Recall the definition of addiction,

01:21:54.360 --> 01:21:56.370
a progressive narrowing of the things

01:21:56.370 --> 01:21:57.203
that bring you pleasure.

01:21:57.203 --> 01:22:01.800
We can change that statement
to also say a progressive

01:22:01.800 --> 01:22:05.130
narrowing and specific kinetics,

01:22:05.130 --> 01:22:08.010
that is specific time course of the things

01:22:08.010 --> 01:22:09.210
that bring you pleasure.

01:22:09.210 --> 01:22:10.950
Now that's a bit of a
nerdier statement than

01:22:10.950 --> 01:22:12.090
a progressive narrowing of the things

01:22:12.090 --> 01:22:12.923
that bring you pleasure.

01:22:12.923 --> 01:22:15.570
But what we are going to
see in the next few years,

01:22:15.570 --> 01:22:19.020
I believe is an entire
generation of young people

01:22:19.020 --> 01:22:22.770
for which a very large percentage
of them are going to be

01:22:22.770 --> 01:22:25.560
seeking out a pattern or feeling

01:22:25.560 --> 01:22:26.700
because to the person taking it,

01:22:26.700 --> 01:22:28.230
they're not looking at
their dopamine levels

01:22:28.230 --> 01:22:29.310
rising in their blood.

01:22:29.310 --> 01:22:31.220
They're experiencing this
as an increase in focus,

01:22:31.220 --> 01:22:32.270
an increase in alertness,

01:22:32.270 --> 01:22:36.300
an increase in mood and
wellbeing that is very rapid,

01:22:36.300 --> 01:22:37.590
very dramatic,

01:22:37.590 --> 01:22:41.970
and that simply cannot be
recreated by any other substance.

01:22:41.970 --> 01:22:44.580
And that's a very concerning scenario,

01:22:44.580 --> 01:22:47.640
concerning because that
mesolimbic reward pathway

01:22:47.640 --> 01:22:49.470
is the only pathway,

01:22:49.470 --> 01:22:53.190
the only pathway by which
you can achieve the rewarding

01:22:53.190 --> 01:22:55.560
properties of any kind of endeavor,

01:22:55.560 --> 01:22:57.930
not just vaping of course.

01:22:57.930 --> 01:23:00.930
It is the way in which people
achieve satisfaction from

01:23:00.930 --> 01:23:04.050
reaching a relationship
goal or an athletic goal

01:23:04.050 --> 01:23:05.850
or an educational goal,

01:23:05.850 --> 01:23:08.220
any kind of goal or sense of satisfaction.

01:23:08.220 --> 01:23:11.640
And so it comes as no surprise
that vaping is strongly

01:23:11.640 --> 01:23:14.460
associated with increased
levels of depression,

01:23:14.460 --> 01:23:16.590
especially when vaping use drops off.

01:23:16.590 --> 01:23:17.490
Why would that be?

01:23:17.490 --> 01:23:19.290
If people are getting
so much more dopamine

01:23:19.290 --> 01:23:20.700
so much more quickly from vaping,

01:23:20.700 --> 01:23:22.023
shouldn't they be feeling better?

01:23:22.023 --> 01:23:25.470
That brings us back to the
critical, and I should say,

01:23:25.470 --> 01:23:28.110
defining feature of the
dopamine reward pathway

01:23:28.110 --> 01:23:29.910
for motivation and wellbeing,

01:23:29.910 --> 01:23:33.120
which is that initially a
given substance will cause,

01:23:33.120 --> 01:23:35.940
let's say an eight out of
10, again, arbitrary units,

01:23:35.940 --> 01:23:39.150
but eight out of 10 increase in dopamine.

01:23:39.150 --> 01:23:40.560
But with repeated use,

01:23:40.560 --> 01:23:43.530
what ends up happening
is that even at similar

01:23:43.530 --> 01:23:45.330
or higher levels of ingestion,

01:23:45.330 --> 01:23:47.400
so not just one inhalation a minute,

01:23:47.400 --> 01:23:48.700
but maybe two inhalations a minute

01:23:48.700 --> 01:23:51.570
or an inhalation every 30 seconds.

01:23:51.570 --> 01:23:54.180
Now it's level four out of 10 activation

01:23:54.180 --> 01:23:55.800
of dopamine and then three and then two.

01:23:55.800 --> 01:23:58.080
And eventually it drops below baseline.

01:23:58.080 --> 01:23:59.370
Now the whole system can be recovered

01:23:59.370 --> 01:24:02.790
by abstinence from nicotine consumption,

01:24:02.790 --> 01:24:05.010
but the dopamine system
is applied to everything,

01:24:05.010 --> 01:24:07.620
all sense of wellbeing,
all sense of motivation.

01:24:07.620 --> 01:24:10.350
So during the period in
which one is abstaining

01:24:10.350 --> 01:24:12.843
from nicotine and vaping in particular,

01:24:13.770 --> 01:24:15.120
people do not feel good.

01:24:15.120 --> 01:24:17.160
They feel miserable. That's
why it's called withdrawal.

01:24:17.160 --> 01:24:20.610
It's associated with a
bunch of psychological and

01:24:20.610 --> 01:24:24.060
physiological symptoms that all lead back

01:24:24.060 --> 01:24:27.600
to trying to get to the same
levels of dopamine release

01:24:27.600 --> 01:24:29.940
in the same patterns that were experienced

01:24:29.940 --> 01:24:32.160
when one initially started vaping.

01:24:32.160 --> 01:24:34.650
So I'm not trying to paint a
dark picture here, but frankly,

01:24:34.650 --> 01:24:36.630
the picture is very dark.

01:24:36.630 --> 01:24:40.350
I'm very concerned about a
practice of ingesting something

01:24:40.350 --> 01:24:43.350
that powerfully modulates the
dopamine system with the sorts

01:24:43.350 --> 01:24:45.990
of kinetics that one sees from
ingestion of crack cocaine

01:24:45.990 --> 01:24:48.300
in young people that has all
these other negative health

01:24:48.300 --> 01:24:51.300
effects that in the short term
is very powerfully rewarding,

01:24:51.300 --> 01:24:53.490
increased focus, wellbeing, et cetera,

01:24:53.490 --> 01:24:57.120
but that over a very brief
period of time leads to a state

01:24:57.120 --> 01:25:01.920
in which the entire dopamine
reward system is impaired and

01:25:01.920 --> 01:25:03.840
can become permanently impaired

01:25:03.840 --> 01:25:06.870
unless one intervenes in
a very deliberate way.

01:25:06.870 --> 01:25:09.990
So people are directly
controlling the rate of dopamine

01:25:09.990 --> 01:25:12.960
and the frequency of dopamine
release by the duration

01:25:12.960 --> 01:25:16.800
and frequency of inhalations
off the vape pen.

01:25:16.800 --> 01:25:20.340
And that's an incredible thing
because what it does is it

01:25:20.340 --> 01:25:23.430
sets up the mesolimbic reward
pathway for an expectation

01:25:23.430 --> 01:25:26.610
that dopamine will be
delivered on that schedule.

01:25:26.610 --> 01:25:28.290
So that's one of the things

01:25:28.290 --> 01:25:32.569
that makes vaping in
particular so hard to quit.

01:25:32.569 --> 01:25:34.860
Vaping is actually harder to quit

01:25:34.860 --> 01:25:37.335
than cigarette smoking for most people.

01:25:37.335 --> 01:25:39.570
Now does that mean that cigarette smoking

01:25:39.570 --> 01:25:41.940
is fairly easy to quit for most people?

01:25:41.940 --> 01:25:43.290
No.

01:25:43.290 --> 01:25:45.360
70% of people who smoke cigarettes report

01:25:45.360 --> 01:25:48.900
that they would like to quit
if they thought they could.

01:25:48.900 --> 01:25:51.150
The success rate of quitting smoking

01:25:51.150 --> 01:25:53.160
when people try to go just cold turkey,

01:25:53.160 --> 01:25:54.930
just quit with no assistance whatsoever.

01:25:54.930 --> 01:25:56.250
They might tell their family and friends,

01:25:56.250 --> 01:25:57.700
hey, I'm quitting, that's it,

01:25:58.602 --> 01:26:01.410
is exceedingly low, it's 5%.

01:26:01.410 --> 01:26:04.860
So 5% of the people that say, that's it,

01:26:04.860 --> 01:26:06.030
I'm not smoking again.

01:26:06.030 --> 01:26:08.970
Despite cancer diagnosis,
I'm not smoking again.

01:26:08.970 --> 01:26:10.710
Despite the fear of the
negative health effects,

01:26:10.710 --> 01:26:12.690
I'm not going to ever smoke again.

01:26:12.690 --> 01:26:16.898
Despite the financial
cost, the health cost.

01:26:16.898 --> 01:26:18.793
I could list off a huge
number of things that it does

01:26:18.793 --> 01:26:19.626
that are negative,

01:26:19.626 --> 01:26:21.840
but you already know these
or you've heard them,

01:26:21.840 --> 01:26:25.770
it makes your skin worse, as
I mentioned it lowers libido,

01:26:25.770 --> 01:26:26.880
it disrupts hormones,

01:26:26.880 --> 01:26:30.630
it disrupts vascular
function, brain function.

01:26:30.630 --> 01:26:31.830
It does all these terrible things.

01:26:31.830 --> 01:26:34.290
And yet most people who
try and quit simply can't.

01:26:34.290 --> 01:26:36.990
And of the 5% that succeed in quitting,

01:26:36.990 --> 01:26:40.590
a full 65% of them relapse within a year.

01:26:40.590 --> 01:26:42.900
So that's a very depressing picture,

01:26:42.900 --> 01:26:44.787
but it's not to say
that people cannot quit.

01:26:44.787 --> 01:26:45.990
And in fact, they can.

01:26:45.990 --> 01:26:47.010
There are a couple of methods

01:26:47.010 --> 01:26:49.440
that have been shown to help people quit.

01:26:49.440 --> 01:26:53.070
Some are behavioral and
some are pharmacologic.

01:26:53.070 --> 01:26:55.710
I just want to touch on the
behavioral ones first because it

01:26:55.710 --> 01:26:58.860
turns out that there's
a quite powerful method

01:26:58.860 --> 01:27:02.790
for quitting nicotine ingestion
by way of cigarette smoking,

01:27:02.790 --> 01:27:04.770
which also carries over to vaping.

01:27:04.770 --> 01:27:07.530
This is beautiful work that's
been done by my colleague,

01:27:07.530 --> 01:27:08.760
in fact, close collaborator,

01:27:08.760 --> 01:27:10.260
although I was not
involved in the research

01:27:10.260 --> 01:27:12.210
that I'm about to describe at Stanford.

01:27:12.210 --> 01:27:13.880
And his name is Dr. David Spiegel.

01:27:13.880 --> 01:27:16.590
He is our associate chair of psychiatry.

01:27:16.590 --> 01:27:18.570
He's been a guest on the
Huberman Lab Podcast,

01:27:18.570 --> 01:27:20.160
and he is a world expert,

01:27:20.160 --> 01:27:21.840
if not the world expert

01:27:21.840 --> 01:27:24.180
in the clinical applications of hypnosis.

01:27:24.180 --> 01:27:25.290
Now when I say hypnosis,

01:27:25.290 --> 01:27:27.000
a lot of people think stage hypnosis,

01:27:27.000 --> 01:27:27.900
which is the hypnotist

01:27:27.900 --> 01:27:29.730
trying to get people to do certain things

01:27:29.730 --> 01:27:30.960
and say certain things,

01:27:30.960 --> 01:27:32.940
not necessarily against their will

01:27:32.940 --> 01:27:34.170
because they actually have to agree.

01:27:34.170 --> 01:27:36.030
But the hypnotist is dictating

01:27:36.030 --> 01:27:38.880
what the person thinks, says, and does.

01:27:38.880 --> 01:27:41.910
Clinical hypnosis is
vastly different from that.

01:27:41.910 --> 01:27:44.460
Clinical hypnosis is where the person,

01:27:44.460 --> 01:27:48.330
the patient actually directs
their own brain changes toward

01:27:48.330 --> 01:27:51.150
a specific emotional or behavioral goal.

01:27:51.150 --> 01:27:55.380
Work from Dr. David Spiegel's
laboratory done in 1993,

01:27:55.380 --> 01:27:57.030
but that now has been repeated many,

01:27:57.030 --> 01:27:59.160
many times that has carried
over into some more modern

01:27:59.160 --> 01:28:01.950
studies and I will provide
links to those studies

01:28:01.950 --> 01:28:04.770
in the show note captions
so that you can access them.

01:28:04.770 --> 01:28:06.240
Those studies have shown that

01:28:06.240 --> 01:28:08.943
using a specific form of hypnosis,

01:28:09.840 --> 01:28:12.630
people can achieve complete

01:28:12.630 --> 01:28:15.720
and total cessation of cigarette smoking.

01:28:15.720 --> 01:28:17.910
And there's no reason to
believe this doesn't also carry

01:28:17.910 --> 01:28:21.780
over to vaping through one
single hypnosis session.

01:28:21.780 --> 01:28:25.230
And the success rates are
incredibly high when one considers

01:28:25.230 --> 01:28:28.290
that normally it would be
only a 5% success rate.

01:28:28.290 --> 01:28:31.170
The success rate with this
particular hypnosis developed

01:28:31.170 --> 01:28:32.310
at Stanford School of Medicine

01:28:32.310 --> 01:28:35.580
by Dr. David Spiegel is 23%

01:28:35.580 --> 01:28:38.010
of people who do this hypnosis one time

01:28:38.010 --> 01:28:39.630
succeed in quitting smoking.

01:28:39.630 --> 01:28:41.820
Now in the old days,

01:28:41.820 --> 01:28:43.620
which actually wasn't that long ago,

01:28:43.620 --> 01:28:45.720
before the advent of smartphones

01:28:45.720 --> 01:28:47.790
and before the internet took off

01:28:47.790 --> 01:28:49.383
to the extent that it has now,

01:28:50.370 --> 01:28:53.633
this was done by having
someone come into the clinic

01:28:53.633 --> 01:28:54.466
and Dr. Spiegel himself

01:28:54.466 --> 01:28:56.130
or one of his colleagues
would take somebody

01:28:56.130 --> 01:28:57.180
through the hypnosis.

01:28:58.290 --> 01:29:00.600
Nowadays, you can access this hypnosis.

01:29:00.600 --> 01:29:02.970
There's a wonderful app that was developed

01:29:02.970 --> 01:29:05.280
by Dr. David Spiegel and others.

01:29:05.280 --> 01:29:07.860
It's called Reveri, R-E-V-E-R-I.

01:29:07.860 --> 01:29:09.090
I've talked about this app a few times

01:29:09.090 --> 01:29:10.050
on the podcast before,

01:29:10.050 --> 01:29:12.480
because there are hypnosis
scripts within the app for

01:29:12.480 --> 01:29:14.940
enhancing sleep, for improving
ability of fall asleep

01:29:14.940 --> 01:29:17.190
if you wake up in the middle
of the night, for focus,

01:29:17.190 --> 01:29:20.550
and a number of other behavioral
and emotional changes.

01:29:20.550 --> 01:29:23.040
There's also a function in the Reveri app

01:29:23.040 --> 01:29:24.690
for smoking cessation,

01:29:24.690 --> 01:29:28.920
which exactly parallels
the sort of in laboratory

01:29:28.920 --> 01:29:31.980
and in clinic approaches
that Dr. Spiegel would use

01:29:31.980 --> 01:29:34.470
were you to show up at his
clinic or his laboratory.

01:29:34.470 --> 01:29:35.940
And since that's not possible

01:29:35.940 --> 01:29:37.890
for the large number of people out there,

01:29:37.890 --> 01:29:40.740
if you or somebody else
is trying to quit smoking

01:29:40.740 --> 01:29:45.180
or vaping or dipping or
snuffing for that matter,

01:29:45.180 --> 01:29:47.880
I strongly encourage you to
check out the Reveri app.

01:29:47.880 --> 01:29:50.820
You can find it easily
by going to reveri.com.

01:29:50.820 --> 01:29:53.508
It's available in various formats.

01:29:53.508 --> 01:29:54.341
Some of it is available free,

01:29:54.341 --> 01:29:55.620
some of it is behind a paywall,

01:29:55.620 --> 01:29:57.930
but given the tremendously negative impact

01:29:57.930 --> 01:30:00.453
of smoking, vaping, dipping, and snuffing,

01:30:01.290 --> 01:30:05.790
the hypnosis for smoking
cessation that Reveri has seems

01:30:05.790 --> 01:30:09.630
at least to me as a very
powerful and worthwhile resource.

01:30:09.630 --> 01:30:10.620
So please check that out

01:30:10.620 --> 01:30:13.890
if you're somebody who's trying
to quit ingesting nicotine

01:30:13.890 --> 01:30:16.620
by any of the four methods
that I just described.

01:30:16.620 --> 01:30:17.453
Now of course,

01:30:17.453 --> 01:30:18.480
there are other methods
that people have used to

01:30:18.480 --> 01:30:20.280
successfully quit smoking or vaping

01:30:20.280 --> 01:30:23.010
or other forms of nicotine delivery.

01:30:23.010 --> 01:30:25.410
And there's actually an
excellent review on this topic.

01:30:25.410 --> 01:30:27.780
So before diving into a few
of the specifics about some of

01:30:27.780 --> 01:30:31.260
the pharmacology of using nicotine itself

01:30:31.260 --> 01:30:34.080
to quit smoking or nicotine
itself to quit vaping,

01:30:34.080 --> 01:30:36.690
or the use of various things, even SSRIs,

01:30:36.690 --> 01:30:40.410
antidepressants to quit smoking or vaping.

01:30:40.410 --> 01:30:42.510
I just want to point you to a
review article that if you'd

01:30:42.510 --> 01:30:45.000
like to get a complete
survey of all the options

01:30:45.000 --> 01:30:47.730
that are available, there's
an excellent review on this.

01:30:47.730 --> 01:30:49.857
It was published just a
couple of years ago in 2020.

01:30:49.857 --> 01:30:51.157
And the title of the article is

01:30:51.157 --> 01:30:53.910
"Pharmacologic Approach
to Smoking Cessation:

01:30:53.910 --> 01:30:56.580
An Updated Review for
Daily Clinical Practice".

01:30:56.580 --> 01:30:59.880
And even though this is mainly
focused on smoking cessation,

01:30:59.880 --> 01:31:02.430
it carries over quite nicely to vaping.

01:31:02.430 --> 01:31:04.290
And it details a number of statistics,

01:31:04.290 --> 01:31:05.700
the fact that every year,

01:31:05.700 --> 01:31:07.500
700,000 or more people die

01:31:07.500 --> 01:31:08.970
because of smoking related diseases.

01:31:08.970 --> 01:31:09.803
So there again you have

01:31:09.803 --> 01:31:13.380
the negative health effects
that younger people are smoking,

01:31:13.380 --> 01:31:14.970
that women are smoking more nowadays.

01:31:14.970 --> 01:31:16.410
And that even though you see less smoking,

01:31:16.410 --> 01:31:19.140
typically in the US and Canada,
and even in Northern Europe,

01:31:19.140 --> 01:31:21.450
some places, there are still
many many people are smoking

01:31:21.450 --> 01:31:24.840
who would like to quit,
but that 75% of people,

01:31:24.840 --> 01:31:27.030
at least according to this
review earlier, I said 70%,

01:31:27.030 --> 01:31:30.180
but estimates are as high as
75% of people who try to quit

01:31:30.180 --> 01:31:32.760
smoking relapse within the first week.

01:31:32.760 --> 01:31:34.290
The first week, they
just go right back to it.

01:31:34.290 --> 01:31:36.810
That's how powerfully
reinforcing the nicotine is.

01:31:36.810 --> 01:31:38.880
Remember, it's the nicotine
in the cigarette that's

01:31:38.880 --> 01:31:43.880
powerfully reinforcing, but
it's also the oral habit,

01:31:44.010 --> 01:31:45.360
the motor habit.

01:31:45.360 --> 01:31:46.830
There is this thing about density

01:31:46.830 --> 01:31:48.240
of sensory receptors in the lips.

01:31:48.240 --> 01:31:50.613
People like bringing things to their lips,

01:31:51.480 --> 01:31:56.480
food, cigarettes, other lips
in some cases, et cetera,

01:31:56.610 --> 01:31:59.010
there is a reinforcement
pathway related to that

01:31:59.010 --> 01:32:01.470
for sort of obvious adaptive reasons.

01:32:01.470 --> 01:32:05.490
And as a consequence,
there is a reinforcement,

01:32:05.490 --> 01:32:07.590
both from the behavior
and from the dopamine

01:32:07.590 --> 01:32:09.180
released from the nicotine itself.

01:32:09.180 --> 01:32:10.560
And as I mentioned earlier,

01:32:10.560 --> 01:32:12.720
from the positive reinforcement

01:32:12.720 --> 01:32:13.950
that comes from increased focus.

01:32:13.950 --> 01:32:15.300
So the money that you make through work

01:32:15.300 --> 01:32:16.680
or your attentional ability

01:32:16.680 --> 01:32:18.630
or the fact that you're alert
and people feel you present,

01:32:18.630 --> 01:32:21.900
all of that funnels back
into positive reinforcement,

01:32:21.900 --> 01:32:23.790
behavioral reinforcement,

01:32:23.790 --> 01:32:26.280
and then what we would call addiction.

01:32:26.280 --> 01:32:30.240
So this review covers all of
that and then steps beautifully

01:32:30.240 --> 01:32:33.840
through nicotine replacement
therapy and various compounds,

01:32:33.840 --> 01:32:35.760
several of which I'm
going to talk about now,

01:32:35.760 --> 01:32:38.430
which have been shown to increase
that number that we talked

01:32:38.430 --> 01:32:41.490
about earlier of only 5%
of people who try to quit

01:32:41.490 --> 01:32:44.430
with no other support
pharmacologic or hypnosis

01:32:44.430 --> 01:32:47.070
or otherwise just say that's it,
I'm not going to smoke again,

01:32:47.070 --> 01:32:48.690
or I'm not going to vape again,

01:32:48.690 --> 01:32:50.940
only 5% of people succeed in doing that.

01:32:50.940 --> 01:32:53.790
And even among those, many
end up relapsing later,

01:32:53.790 --> 01:32:55.890
there are a couple
pharmacologic approaches.

01:32:55.890 --> 01:32:57.930
One of the main ones that's
received a lot of attention in

01:32:57.930 --> 01:32:59.230
recent years is Bupropion,

01:33:00.120 --> 01:33:04.110
sometimes referred to by its
commercial name Wellbutrin.

01:33:04.110 --> 01:33:09.110
Now Bupropion is a compound
that increases the release of

01:33:09.270 --> 01:33:11.940
dopamine and to a lesser
extent epinephrine

01:33:11.940 --> 01:33:13.620
and some other neurochemicals as well.

01:33:13.620 --> 01:33:15.660
It's used for the treatment of depression

01:33:15.660 --> 01:33:17.730
and for smoking cessation.

01:33:17.730 --> 01:33:19.980
Now I want to point out
again, I'm not a psychiatrist,

01:33:19.980 --> 01:33:23.490
so I'm not telling you to take
Bupropion, AKA Wellbutrin,

01:33:23.490 --> 01:33:25.500
but I'm going to give you a little
bit of the contour of what's

01:33:25.500 --> 01:33:28.500
typically done in terms of
Bupropion administration

01:33:28.500 --> 01:33:31.410
to help people get relief
from some of the withdrawal

01:33:31.410 --> 01:33:33.930
symptoms of trying to
quit smoking or vaping

01:33:33.930 --> 01:33:36.570
or other forms of nicotine ingestion.

01:33:36.570 --> 01:33:41.010
Typically Bupropion is taken
in 300 milligram per day

01:33:41.010 --> 01:33:44.190
doses divided into two dosages
of 150 milligrams each,

01:33:44.190 --> 01:33:47.250
or sometimes there's a
slow release formula.

01:33:47.250 --> 01:33:49.950
The dosages will vary
from person to person.

01:33:49.950 --> 01:33:51.980
I want to really emphasize that there

01:33:51.980 --> 01:33:54.690
is an increased seizure
risk with Bupropion.

01:33:54.690 --> 01:33:56.970
It only occurs in a small
fraction of the population,

01:33:56.970 --> 01:33:58.980
but nonetheless is a real concern

01:33:58.980 --> 01:34:00.420
for those members of the population.

01:34:00.420 --> 01:34:02.220
So for those of you with seizure risk,

01:34:02.220 --> 01:34:03.660
whether you know it or not,

01:34:03.660 --> 01:34:06.180
that's going to be a valid concern

01:34:06.180 --> 01:34:08.010
in terms of potential side effects.

01:34:08.010 --> 01:34:11.760
The other thing about Bupropion
is that it has to be used

01:34:11.760 --> 01:34:14.160
with caution in patients that
have liver disease or renal

01:34:14.160 --> 01:34:16.620
disease that can impact the amount

01:34:16.620 --> 01:34:18.720
that anyone can take meaning
sometimes people have to take

01:34:18.720 --> 01:34:20.790
a much lower dose if
they have renal disease

01:34:20.790 --> 01:34:22.230
or liver disease.

01:34:22.230 --> 01:34:23.940
And sometimes they can't take it at all.

01:34:23.940 --> 01:34:26.670
Sometimes if people are taking
benzodiazepines for whatever

01:34:26.670 --> 01:34:28.143
reason or other sedatives,

01:34:28.994 --> 01:34:32.169
there are contraindications
there, so Bupropion isn't a

01:34:32.169 --> 01:34:34.380
kind of one size fits all or magic bullet

01:34:34.380 --> 01:34:36.420
for quitting smoking, nonetheless,

01:34:36.420 --> 01:34:38.070
for people that can take it safely,

01:34:38.070 --> 01:34:39.900
and again, this is a prescription drug,

01:34:39.900 --> 01:34:42.510
a board certified psychiatrist
or other physician is going

01:34:42.510 --> 01:34:44.640
to have to prescribe it for you
if it's appropriate for you.

01:34:44.640 --> 01:34:48.240
And it moves that number of
5% success rate to about what

01:34:48.240 --> 01:34:53.240
one sees with the clinical
hypnosis to about 20% of people

01:34:53.370 --> 01:34:56.790
will successfully overcome their nicotine,

01:34:56.790 --> 01:35:00.300
or I should say their
smoking or vaping addiction.

01:35:00.300 --> 01:35:03.540
Now it's important to ask
why this would work, right?

01:35:03.540 --> 01:35:08.250
It's not as if Bupropion is
increasing nicotine per se.

01:35:08.250 --> 01:35:09.600
What it's doing is it's tapping

01:35:09.600 --> 01:35:12.330
on that mesolimbic reward
pathway, increasing dopamine,

01:35:12.330 --> 01:35:15.180
or at least allowing dopamine
levels to say substantially

01:35:15.180 --> 01:35:18.990
elevated enough that people
don't experience some of the

01:35:18.990 --> 01:35:21.420
drop in dopamine that leads
to the withdrawal symptoms,

01:35:21.420 --> 01:35:22.860
the lessening of mood, et cetera.

01:35:22.860 --> 01:35:24.660
And it's no coincidence that Bupropion

01:35:24.660 --> 01:35:26.430
is also an antidepressant.

01:35:26.430 --> 01:35:28.950
It's a common antidepressant
for people that experience

01:35:28.950 --> 01:35:31.290
negative side effects
with the so-called SSRIs,

01:35:31.290 --> 01:35:34.500
the selective serotonin
reuptake inhibitors that prevent

01:35:34.500 --> 01:35:36.510
them from taking those
things like lessen libido

01:35:36.510 --> 01:35:37.343
or appetite,

01:35:37.343 --> 01:35:39.900
or in some cases increased
appetite or any number

01:35:39.900 --> 01:35:42.060
of other side effects
that some people, not all,

01:35:42.060 --> 01:35:43.830
but some people experience with SSRIs.

01:35:43.830 --> 01:35:45.270
They'll be prescribed Wellbutrin,

01:35:45.270 --> 01:35:48.180
Bupropion is the generic name.

01:35:48.180 --> 01:35:50.190
So Wellbutrin being the
commercial name again,

01:35:50.190 --> 01:35:54.510
Bupropion is what they'll be
prescribed instead with the

01:35:54.510 --> 01:35:57.600
caveats of seizure risk, renal disease,

01:35:57.600 --> 01:35:58.923
liver disease, et cetera.

01:35:59.760 --> 01:36:02.460
The outcomes with Wellbutrin
for smoking cessation

01:36:02.460 --> 01:36:04.020
are pretty good if you
think about an increase

01:36:04.020 --> 01:36:07.110
from 5% to 20%, that's pretty dramatic.

01:36:07.110 --> 01:36:10.380
And yet I also want to refer
back to the incredible success

01:36:10.380 --> 01:36:12.120
of the clinical hypnosis approach.

01:36:12.120 --> 01:36:14.730
Again, you can find that at reveri.com,

01:36:14.730 --> 01:36:19.163
the clinical hypnosis approach
has a success rate of 23%.

01:36:20.126 --> 01:36:20.959
So it's very closely aligned with,

01:36:20.959 --> 01:36:24.420
if not exceeding the
success rate with Bupropion.

01:36:24.420 --> 01:36:26.580
Of course there are other
pharmacologic approaches

01:36:26.580 --> 01:36:29.610
to quitting smoking or vaping.

01:36:29.610 --> 01:36:33.060
All of them generally circle
back to increasing dopamine

01:36:33.060 --> 01:36:35.250
and/or norepinephrine in order to offset

01:36:35.250 --> 01:36:38.460
some of the withdrawal
symptoms of smoking cessation

01:36:38.460 --> 01:36:39.720
or vaping cessation.

01:36:39.720 --> 01:36:42.900
A very common approach for
people to try and quit smoking or

01:36:42.900 --> 01:36:47.900
vaping is to use nicotine
itself to try and prevent people

01:36:48.450 --> 01:36:52.290
from seeking nicotine through
a cigarette or a vape pen.

01:36:52.290 --> 01:36:55.440
What I mean by that is people
using a nicotine patch or

01:36:55.440 --> 01:36:59.070
nicotine gum or other
nicotine delivery device

01:36:59.070 --> 01:37:02.010
that is not cigarettes and not vaping

01:37:02.010 --> 01:37:05.250
in order to maintain levels of nicotine

01:37:05.250 --> 01:37:07.110
in their bloodstream,

01:37:07.110 --> 01:37:09.510
which of course means maintain
levels of nicotine in their

01:37:09.510 --> 01:37:12.180
brain and body to the same extent

01:37:12.180 --> 01:37:15.030
that they would if they
were smoking or vaping,

01:37:15.030 --> 01:37:18.570
maybe even gradually taking
down the total amount of

01:37:18.570 --> 01:37:21.630
nicotine in their brain
and body by reducing

01:37:21.630 --> 01:37:24.960
the number or size of nicotine gum pieces

01:37:24.960 --> 01:37:26.430
that they ingest each day,

01:37:26.430 --> 01:37:29.040
or keeping the patch on for
a shorter amount of time

01:37:29.040 --> 01:37:31.770
or getting a lower dose patch
that releases less nicotine

01:37:31.770 --> 01:37:34.353
total or over time.

01:37:35.490 --> 01:37:37.230
All of those approaches have been shown

01:37:37.230 --> 01:37:39.120
to be reasonably successful,

01:37:39.120 --> 01:37:40.230
I'll get to the numbers in a few minutes,

01:37:40.230 --> 01:37:43.350
but reasonably successful
in allowing people

01:37:43.350 --> 01:37:46.750
to quit smoking or vaping.

01:37:46.750 --> 01:37:49.770
Again, most of the data
is on cigarette smoking

01:37:49.770 --> 01:37:52.230
because vaping is a
relatively new phenomenon.

01:37:52.230 --> 01:37:54.480
Although quite troublingly,

01:37:54.480 --> 01:37:57.810
it's a very rapidly increasing behavior,

01:37:57.810 --> 01:37:59.340
especially in the young population.

01:37:59.340 --> 01:38:01.470
So that's why I'm kind of
lumping these two things together

01:38:01.470 --> 01:38:06.210
because I think very soon we
are going to need an all out

01:38:06.210 --> 01:38:09.600
campaign for how to
counter vaping addiction.

01:38:09.600 --> 01:38:11.940
So what do we know about smoking cessation

01:38:11.940 --> 01:38:13.560
using nicotine itself?

01:38:13.560 --> 01:38:16.530
Is the patch best? Is nicotine gum best?

01:38:16.530 --> 01:38:19.680
Turns out that a combination of approaches

01:38:19.680 --> 01:38:21.660
is best, so somewhat surprising,

01:38:21.660 --> 01:38:23.490
but it was very clear from the
literature that I was able to

01:38:23.490 --> 01:38:27.390
find that using nicotine
patches for some period of time

01:38:27.390 --> 01:38:29.250
and then switching to a gum

01:38:29.250 --> 01:38:33.150
and then perhaps switching
to a nasal spray,

01:38:33.150 --> 01:38:34.980
that's going to be the most effective.

01:38:34.980 --> 01:38:38.130
Then the question is how long
to continue each of those

01:38:38.130 --> 01:38:39.600
and whether or not to overlap them.

01:38:39.600 --> 01:38:42.600
It seems as if doing one for
about a week and then switching

01:38:42.600 --> 01:38:45.330
to another for about a week and
then switching to another is

01:38:45.330 --> 01:38:47.820
one rationable and reasonable approach

01:38:47.820 --> 01:38:50.190
that many people have used successfully.

01:38:50.190 --> 01:38:51.090
Why would that be?

01:38:51.090 --> 01:38:53.880
It all has to do with the
different rates of absorption

01:38:53.880 --> 01:38:55.530
of nicotine into the bloodstream,

01:38:55.530 --> 01:38:57.150
and then the downstream consequences

01:38:57.150 --> 01:39:00.480
of that on the dopamine,
acetylcholine, epinephrine,

01:39:00.480 --> 01:39:02.640
and other systems of the brain and body.

01:39:02.640 --> 01:39:05.970
And while there hasn't been
an extremely detailed study

01:39:05.970 --> 01:39:07.860
of the exact kinetics of

01:39:07.860 --> 01:39:10.260
how the nasal sprays versus
the transdermal patches

01:39:10.260 --> 01:39:12.090
versus the gums, et cetera, work,

01:39:12.090 --> 01:39:13.560
there's a logical structure to it

01:39:13.560 --> 01:39:14.880
that will immediately make sense to you.

01:39:14.880 --> 01:39:16.323
First of all,

01:39:16.323 --> 01:39:18.930
the transdermal patches
provide a fairly steady state

01:39:18.930 --> 01:39:21.450
dopamine release across the day.

01:39:21.450 --> 01:39:23.790
And oftentimes people are
wearing them at night as well.

01:39:23.790 --> 01:39:26.850
This is relevant because if
people are ingesting nicotine

01:39:26.850 --> 01:39:29.313
by way of smoking and vaping,

01:39:30.360 --> 01:39:31.950
hopefully they're not waking
up in the middle of the night

01:39:31.950 --> 01:39:32.910
just to smoke or vape.

01:39:32.910 --> 01:39:34.200
Believe or not, some people do that,

01:39:34.200 --> 01:39:36.390
but of course, while people are asleep,

01:39:36.390 --> 01:39:38.550
they are not smoking or vaping.

01:39:38.550 --> 01:39:39.480
They always tell you don't fall asleep

01:39:39.480 --> 01:39:40.313
with a cigarette in your mouth,

01:39:40.313 --> 01:39:42.330
you'll burn the whole house down,

01:39:42.330 --> 01:39:45.510
but exceedingly rare to have people

01:39:45.510 --> 01:39:47.040
are smoking in their sleep.

01:39:47.040 --> 01:39:50.100
So people wake up in the morning
and because the half life

01:39:50.100 --> 01:39:52.110
of nicotine from smoking
or vaping is very short,

01:39:52.110 --> 01:39:54.030
anywhere from one to two hours,

01:39:54.030 --> 01:39:55.980
they're essentially in a state
of withdrawal at the point

01:39:55.980 --> 01:39:57.030
where they wake up in the morning.

01:39:57.030 --> 01:39:57.900
How can I say that?

01:39:57.900 --> 01:40:01.320
Remember, withdrawal sets in
about four hours after the last

01:40:01.320 --> 01:40:03.210
ingestion of nicotine by cigarette

01:40:03.210 --> 01:40:05.100
or by inhalation from the vape pen.

01:40:05.100 --> 01:40:08.100
So people are waking up in
nicotine withdrawal and then

01:40:08.100 --> 01:40:10.950
immediately going into the
behavior of ingesting nicotine,

01:40:10.950 --> 01:40:13.620
or very soon after waking for most people.

01:40:13.620 --> 01:40:15.660
So nicotine patch is going to be

01:40:15.660 --> 01:40:17.910
very effective for a week or so, again,

01:40:17.910 --> 01:40:20.520
talk to your physician about
the best approach for this,

01:40:20.520 --> 01:40:23.940
but then switching to a nasal spray

01:40:23.940 --> 01:40:26.160
or switching to nicotine
gum for about a week,

01:40:26.160 --> 01:40:28.530
which is going to change the
kinetics of that nicotine

01:40:28.530 --> 01:40:31.590
absorption into the bloodstream
and change the release

01:40:31.590 --> 01:40:33.810
of dopamine and other
neurochemicals within the brain.

01:40:33.810 --> 01:40:37.230
That's going to keep the system
intentionally off balance

01:40:37.230 --> 01:40:40.890
so that it never comes to
expect one single pattern

01:40:40.890 --> 01:40:44.040
or amplitude of dopamine release.

01:40:44.040 --> 01:40:47.673
And that is a very powerful way for a,

01:40:48.570 --> 01:40:51.750
let's just call it a
quitting method to work.

01:40:51.750 --> 01:40:53.370
Because as I've always said,

01:40:53.370 --> 01:40:56.280
the most powerful schedule of dopamine

01:40:56.280 --> 01:40:59.190
is going to be this random
intermittent reward.

01:40:59.190 --> 01:41:00.900
This is what's used in the casinos

01:41:00.900 --> 01:41:02.100
in order to take your money.

01:41:02.100 --> 01:41:03.780
And generally they do, on average,

01:41:03.780 --> 01:41:06.360
they take your money more
than you take theirs,

01:41:06.360 --> 01:41:08.100
and they take more of it.

01:41:08.100 --> 01:41:09.660
Not just more often because

01:41:09.660 --> 01:41:11.850
they use this random
intermittent schedule.

01:41:11.850 --> 01:41:14.190
The random intermittent schedule
is one in which you don't

01:41:14.190 --> 01:41:17.340
really know when the peaks in
dopamine are going to arrive.

01:41:17.340 --> 01:41:19.830
And so there isn't this
expectation and craving.

01:41:19.830 --> 01:41:21.510
And then all of a sudden
when dopamine is released,

01:41:21.510 --> 01:41:22.410
it's extremely high.

01:41:22.410 --> 01:41:23.790
That's how they get you
to continue playing,

01:41:23.790 --> 01:41:25.380
even though basically you're losing money

01:41:25.380 --> 01:41:26.370
and your dopamine is dropping,

01:41:26.370 --> 01:41:29.250
they elevate it every once in a while.

01:41:29.250 --> 01:41:31.770
Nicotine replacement can
be used in a similar way,

01:41:31.770 --> 01:41:32.910
but in a benevolent way,

01:41:32.910 --> 01:41:36.300
in order to help you get
over smoking or vaping

01:41:36.300 --> 01:41:39.090
by keeping the total
amounts of dopamine variable

01:41:39.090 --> 01:41:40.740
around the clock

01:41:40.740 --> 01:41:43.590
and by changing the amount
of dopamine that's released,

01:41:43.590 --> 01:41:47.010
it seems to help people
behaviorally and psychologically

01:41:47.010 --> 01:41:49.410
because they don't come to
expect having a particular amount

01:41:49.410 --> 01:41:51.780
of dopamine in their brain
and blood at any given time.

01:41:51.780 --> 01:41:53.700
And this is an important point

01:41:53.700 --> 01:41:56.850
because it brings us to
this notion of homeostasis.

01:41:56.850 --> 01:41:59.520
Homeostasis is this tendency
for biological systems

01:41:59.520 --> 01:42:01.140
to try and reach equilibrium.

01:42:01.140 --> 01:42:02.610
What goes up, goes down, et cetera.

01:42:02.610 --> 01:42:04.532
And to some extent to the same degree.

01:42:04.532 --> 01:42:05.850
So I'll talk about this right now

01:42:05.850 --> 01:42:09.900
in the context of nicotine use withdrawal,

01:42:09.900 --> 01:42:12.300
and then the period in which people

01:42:12.300 --> 01:42:14.550
no longer crave nicotine.

01:42:14.550 --> 01:42:16.290
So you can imagine that

01:42:16.290 --> 01:42:17.760
if we were to measure your heart rate,

01:42:17.760 --> 01:42:20.040
your blood pressure, and your
overall levels of alertness

01:42:20.040 --> 01:42:23.100
and wellbeing and mood, let's
just give that some value.

01:42:23.100 --> 01:42:25.800
Let's say it runs from zero
to 10, again, arbitrary units.

01:42:25.800 --> 01:42:28.020
Let's just take all those
physiological measures

01:42:28.020 --> 01:42:29.397
and the subjected measure of your mood.

01:42:29.397 --> 01:42:30.610
And let's measure it

01:42:31.530 --> 01:42:35.010
four times an hour across the
day, across the waking hours.

01:42:35.010 --> 01:42:36.960
What we would find is a
line that would kind of

01:42:36.960 --> 01:42:37.793
squiggle a bit,

01:42:37.793 --> 01:42:39.900
maybe a nice text comes
in that you really like,

01:42:39.900 --> 01:42:42.930
maybe you get not so good
news and your kind of

01:42:42.930 --> 01:42:45.360
autonomic arousal is all over the place,

01:42:45.360 --> 01:42:47.550
but on average is kind of a squiggly line

01:42:47.550 --> 01:42:48.840
where it increases in the morning

01:42:48.840 --> 01:42:50.760
because that's typically
when body temperature

01:42:50.760 --> 01:42:52.200
and autonomic arousal increase.

01:42:52.200 --> 01:42:53.280
And then towards the afternoon,

01:42:53.280 --> 01:42:54.300
it's going to come sloping down.

01:42:54.300 --> 01:42:55.140
And then right before sleep,

01:42:55.140 --> 01:42:56.340
there'll be an increase again,

01:42:56.340 --> 01:42:58.110
if you've ever felt that you
kind of run around a lot before

01:42:58.110 --> 01:42:59.310
sleep and then it goes down,

01:42:59.310 --> 01:43:03.570
that's kind of the typical contour
of autonomic arousal, mood,

01:43:03.570 --> 01:43:05.760
et cetera, across the day,

01:43:05.760 --> 01:43:09.660
removing of course life events
and things like psychiatric

01:43:09.660 --> 01:43:12.180
illness and depression and et cetera.

01:43:12.180 --> 01:43:13.950
That's the typical arc of that.

01:43:13.950 --> 01:43:15.810
Now let's superimpose on whatever

01:43:15.810 --> 01:43:18.840
that contour is for you, nicotine.

01:43:18.840 --> 01:43:21.780
So you get a little bolus as we say,

01:43:21.780 --> 01:43:23.970
a little bit of nicotine
from smoking a cigarette

01:43:23.970 --> 01:43:25.680
or from taking an
inhalation on a vape pen,

01:43:25.680 --> 01:43:26.690
what ends up happening is there's

01:43:26.690 --> 01:43:28.830
an increase in blood pressure,
increase in heart rate,

01:43:28.830 --> 01:43:30.390
increase in mood, increase in alertness,

01:43:30.390 --> 01:43:32.333
all the things we talked about earlier.

01:43:33.480 --> 01:43:35.280
Over time,

01:43:35.280 --> 01:43:40.280
the body starts to adjust so
that the baseline upon which

01:43:40.830 --> 01:43:45.360
that nicotine induced increase
in arousal would occur

01:43:45.360 --> 01:43:47.790
is actually reduced, right?

01:43:47.790 --> 01:43:48.623
Why would that be?

01:43:48.623 --> 01:43:51.180
Well, the body and the brain,

01:43:51.180 --> 01:43:53.070
your physiology seeks homeostasis.

01:43:53.070 --> 01:43:56.070
So if there's a big
increase in all those things

01:43:56.070 --> 01:43:57.960
like blood pressure and mood, et cetera,

01:43:57.960 --> 01:44:00.750
typically your baseline
will drop a little bit

01:44:00.750 --> 01:44:02.220
to compensate for that over time,

01:44:02.220 --> 01:44:05.070
after a couple days or even
weeks of ingesting nicotine.

01:44:05.070 --> 01:44:06.180
So let's say you wake up,

01:44:06.180 --> 01:44:08.880
you're typically take an
inhalation off your vape,

01:44:08.880 --> 01:44:10.260
or you have a cigarette around

01:44:10.260 --> 01:44:13.320
nine or 10:00 AM and you do that daily.

01:44:13.320 --> 01:44:15.750
You get used to a certain
level of mood and alertness

01:44:15.750 --> 01:44:17.460
and wellbeing for that time of day.

01:44:17.460 --> 01:44:19.140
And then if you smoke
again in the afternoon,

01:44:19.140 --> 01:44:22.350
let's say you also get accustomed
to a certain level of mood

01:44:22.350 --> 01:44:25.080
and alertness and wellbeing
for that time of day.

01:44:25.080 --> 01:44:26.850
Again, it'll vary
depending on life events,

01:44:26.850 --> 01:44:29.160
but your system sort of gets
used to it and your baseline

01:44:29.160 --> 01:44:31.020
will drop to compensate for those peaks

01:44:31.020 --> 01:44:33.210
so that the peaks aren't
quite as high as they were

01:44:33.210 --> 01:44:35.340
when you first started using nicotine.

01:44:35.340 --> 01:44:36.480
Now you decide to quit.

01:44:36.480 --> 01:44:39.570
So now what we're talking
about is transitioning from the

01:44:39.570 --> 01:44:42.270
consumption to what we're going
to call the withdrawal phase.

01:44:42.270 --> 01:44:44.190
So now what happens is you say,

01:44:44.190 --> 01:44:46.140
that's it, I'm going cold turkey.

01:44:46.140 --> 01:44:47.820
I know there's only a 5% success rate,

01:44:47.820 --> 01:44:50.610
but I'm going to just go cold
turkey or somebody will say,

01:44:50.610 --> 01:44:52.020
no, I'm going to use the Reveri app.

01:44:52.020 --> 01:44:53.760
Or somebody says, no,

01:44:53.760 --> 01:44:56.970
I'm going to use Bupropion or
another method or nicotine patch

01:44:56.970 --> 01:44:58.260
or something of that sort.

01:44:58.260 --> 01:44:59.700
Setting aside the nicotine patch

01:44:59.700 --> 01:45:01.530
or the nicotine delivery device

01:45:01.530 --> 01:45:04.920
and only focusing on
approaches for getting through

01:45:04.920 --> 01:45:09.300
withdrawal that have no
direct effects on nicotine.

01:45:09.300 --> 01:45:10.650
So not using the nicotine patch,

01:45:10.650 --> 01:45:14.160
but say the hypnosis or Bupropion,

01:45:14.160 --> 01:45:15.600
which can increase dopamine,

01:45:15.600 --> 01:45:18.420
but it doesn't increase nicotine directly.

01:45:18.420 --> 01:45:19.470
What happens?

01:45:19.470 --> 01:45:21.063
The day that you quit,

01:45:22.200 --> 01:45:25.140
that homeostatic mechanism
in your brain and body

01:45:25.140 --> 01:45:27.930
that sets your level of
mood and arousal, et cetera,

01:45:27.930 --> 01:45:30.960
does not know and hasn't
adjusted to the fact

01:45:30.960 --> 01:45:32.400
that you're not bringing in nicotine.

01:45:32.400 --> 01:45:33.450
You're not having that cigarette.

01:45:33.450 --> 01:45:35.880
You're not having that
inhalation on the vape pen.

01:45:35.880 --> 01:45:38.130
So what ends up happening
is that baseline,

01:45:38.130 --> 01:45:41.610
which has been adjusted
down to offset the increases

01:45:41.610 --> 01:45:43.710
in mood and alertness, et cetera,

01:45:43.710 --> 01:45:48.300
when you smoke or vape is lower
than it normally would be.

01:45:48.300 --> 01:45:52.950
So that 9:00 AM cigarette
time or vape inhalation time

01:45:52.950 --> 01:45:55.470
no longer feels above baseline,

01:45:55.470 --> 01:45:58.380
it actually feels below baseline
because what you're seeing

01:45:58.380 --> 01:46:03.380
is the lower amplitude
of arousal that was there

01:46:03.690 --> 01:46:05.940
to offset the increase you were getting

01:46:05.940 --> 01:46:07.320
from vaping or smoking.

01:46:07.320 --> 01:46:08.370
And then in the afternoon,

01:46:08.370 --> 01:46:10.530
if normally you have a kind
of phase of your afternoon

01:46:10.530 --> 01:46:11.760
you really enjoy, you go outside,

01:46:11.760 --> 01:46:13.500
you have a vape or a cigarette,

01:46:13.500 --> 01:46:15.706
you normally are feeling
relaxed or you go out at night

01:46:15.706 --> 01:46:16.560
and you like to vape and you say, nope,

01:46:16.560 --> 01:46:18.060
I'm not going to do that anymore,

01:46:18.060 --> 01:46:19.350
you're going to feel much,

01:46:19.350 --> 01:46:22.440
much worse than you would've
had you never started

01:46:22.440 --> 01:46:23.430
smoking or vaping.

01:46:23.430 --> 01:46:25.434
Now that's not much help to anyone

01:46:25.434 --> 01:46:27.810
who's already started smoking or vaping.

01:46:27.810 --> 01:46:30.900
But I say this because it's
very important to understand

01:46:30.900 --> 01:46:32.550
that the reason why relapse rates

01:46:32.550 --> 01:46:34.020
are so high within the first week,

01:46:34.020 --> 01:46:37.980
75% of people relapse within
one week and overall failure

01:46:37.980 --> 01:46:41.340
rates are 95% is because
people don't expect

01:46:41.340 --> 01:46:44.340
to feel even worse than they did prior

01:46:44.340 --> 01:46:46.080
to ever smoking or vaping.

01:46:46.080 --> 01:46:48.780
So that first week is absolutely critical.

01:46:48.780 --> 01:46:50.250
And the beauty of understanding this

01:46:50.250 --> 01:46:52.620
is that if you can get
through that first week,

01:46:52.620 --> 01:46:55.890
either by sheer grit or
by finding other methods

01:46:55.890 --> 01:46:58.830
to increase dopamine,
healthy methods I would hope,

01:46:58.830 --> 01:47:00.540
and certainly cold showers,

01:47:00.540 --> 01:47:03.237
ice baths have been shown
to do that by the way.

01:47:03.237 --> 01:47:05.377
And this was described in
Dr. Anna Lembke's book,

01:47:05.377 --> 01:47:08.940
"Dopamine Nation", cold
showers can increase dopamine,

01:47:08.940 --> 01:47:11.400
exercise, positive social interactions.

01:47:11.400 --> 01:47:13.830
It's very likely that
people will need to use

01:47:13.830 --> 01:47:17.190
other healthy methods to offset
that reduction in dopamine

01:47:17.190 --> 01:47:19.830
if ever they stand to get
through that first week.

01:47:19.830 --> 01:47:22.380
And again, if you can get
through that first week,

01:47:22.380 --> 01:47:25.050
chances are quite a bit
higher that you'll be able to

01:47:25.050 --> 01:47:29.130
maintain the cessation
of smoking or vaping.

01:47:29.130 --> 01:47:32.040
And of course, hypnosis,
things like Bupropion,

01:47:32.040 --> 01:47:33.300
can also assist in that,

01:47:33.300 --> 01:47:36.270
Bupropion by way of increasing
dopamine pharmacologically,

01:47:36.270 --> 01:47:39.120
hypnosis through changes
in neural circuitry

01:47:39.120 --> 01:47:40.380
that aren't completely understood,

01:47:40.380 --> 01:47:43.800
but seem to involve a remapping
of some of the so-called

01:47:43.800 --> 01:47:46.410
default networks and some of
the networks that are involved

01:47:46.410 --> 01:47:48.930
in kind of understanding
of your own internal state.

01:47:48.930 --> 01:47:50.340
This stuff gets a little bit
complicated and we're going to

01:47:50.340 --> 01:47:52.980
return to this in a upcoming episode

01:47:52.980 --> 01:47:54.180
of the Huberman Lab Podcast,

01:47:54.180 --> 01:47:57.780
but there are indeed legitimate
changes in neural circuitry

01:47:57.780 --> 01:48:00.780
caused by clinical hypnosis
that can at least partially

01:48:00.780 --> 01:48:04.380
explain why it is so
effective in helping treat

01:48:04.380 --> 01:48:07.050
or allow people to stop
smoking and vaping.

01:48:07.050 --> 01:48:09.232
So for those of you out there

01:48:09.232 --> 01:48:11.790
that, either here or
are saying yourselves,

01:48:11.790 --> 01:48:15.240
I just can't seem to
quit smoking or vaping

01:48:15.240 --> 01:48:17.310
or dipping or snuffing,

01:48:17.310 --> 01:48:20.940
hopefully an understanding of
how that homeostasis process

01:48:20.940 --> 01:48:23.490
works and the time course of nicotine,

01:48:23.490 --> 01:48:25.200
depending on the delivery device,

01:48:25.200 --> 01:48:26.730
hopefully understanding that

01:48:26.730 --> 01:48:29.910
will allow you to develop a protocol.

01:48:29.910 --> 01:48:30.990
Maybe it involves hypnosis.

01:48:30.990 --> 01:48:33.870
Maybe it involves just
understanding that the typical times

01:48:33.870 --> 01:48:37.920
in which you ingested nicotine
through any of the different

01:48:37.920 --> 01:48:40.050
approaches of bringing it into your system

01:48:40.050 --> 01:48:41.340
are going to be particularly hard.

01:48:41.340 --> 01:48:43.200
But I don't just mean particularly hard.

01:48:43.200 --> 01:48:44.033
I mean, particularly hard,

01:48:44.033 --> 01:48:47.220
and you're going to need to do
something specific to offset

01:48:47.220 --> 01:48:50.580
that decrease in overall autonomic arousal

01:48:50.580 --> 01:48:52.200
and dopamine, et cetera.

01:48:52.200 --> 01:48:53.940
Hopefully an understanding
of that will allow you

01:48:53.940 --> 01:48:55.050
to get through that first week.

01:48:55.050 --> 01:48:56.910
And if you can make it
past that first week,

01:48:56.910 --> 01:48:59.730
you stand a very good
chance of never going back.

01:48:59.730 --> 01:49:02.430
However, I did consult
with Dr. David Spiegel

01:49:02.430 --> 01:49:04.380
in anticipation of this episode,

01:49:04.380 --> 01:49:08.520
regardless of the method
that you used to quit smoking

01:49:08.520 --> 01:49:10.923
or vaping, snuffing, or dipping,

01:49:12.150 --> 01:49:16.620
there's good evidence that
a routine maybe once a month

01:49:16.620 --> 01:49:21.620
or even once a week hypnosis
type approach to replenish

01:49:21.990 --> 01:49:24.660
or even enhance the neural
circuits that are allowing you to

01:49:24.660 --> 01:49:27.180
stay away from nicotine is
going to be a very good idea.

01:49:27.180 --> 01:49:31.440
And given that it's a purely
behavioral intervention,

01:49:31.440 --> 01:49:33.690
I can see no reason as to
why people wouldn't want

01:49:33.690 --> 01:49:35.010
to do that, go in and reinforce, you know,

01:49:35.010 --> 01:49:38.250
tighten the bolts on that
circuitry that are allowing you to

01:49:38.250 --> 01:49:41.580
not feel the impulse to smoke,
not feel the impulse to vape.

01:49:41.580 --> 01:49:43.860
And just a very brief mention,

01:49:43.860 --> 01:49:47.580
there is a vast literature on
the fact that when people have

01:49:47.580 --> 01:49:51.420
quit smoking or vaping or other
form of consuming nicotine,

01:49:51.420 --> 01:49:52.740
that when they consume alcohol,

01:49:52.740 --> 01:49:53.980
there's a much higher
probability of relapse.

01:49:53.980 --> 01:49:56.220
There are interactions
between alcohol and nicotine

01:49:56.220 --> 01:49:57.870
that we'll cover in future episodes.

01:49:57.870 --> 01:49:59.430
But for those of you that want to quit,

01:49:59.430 --> 01:50:00.780
I want to assure you,

01:50:00.780 --> 01:50:03.450
despite the fact that 95% of people fail,

01:50:03.450 --> 01:50:05.040
with the appropriate tools,

01:50:05.040 --> 01:50:07.800
and I like to think within an
additional understanding of

01:50:07.800 --> 01:50:10.110
the underlying biology and
psychology and what you can

01:50:10.110 --> 01:50:12.270
expect and when to really
dig your heels and when to

01:50:12.270 --> 01:50:14.130
reinforce your system with more dopamine

01:50:14.130 --> 01:50:16.200
through any of the number
of the different protocols

01:50:16.200 --> 01:50:17.640
and tools that we've offered here

01:50:17.640 --> 01:50:18.720
and that you can find elsewhere

01:50:18.720 --> 01:50:21.210
in other episodes of the
Huberman Lab Podcast,

01:50:21.210 --> 01:50:25.430
I have a high degree of confidence
that you can quit smoking

01:50:25.430 --> 01:50:27.690
or vaping, dipping or snuffing.

01:50:27.690 --> 01:50:29.310
So today, typical of frankly

01:50:29.310 --> 01:50:30.900
all episodes of the Huberman Lab Podcast,

01:50:30.900 --> 01:50:33.124
we've covered a lot about the biology

01:50:33.124 --> 01:50:34.740
of a particular system.

01:50:34.740 --> 01:50:38.040
We talked about the biology
of nicotine in particular,

01:50:38.040 --> 01:50:42.180
we talked about vaping and
smoking, dipping and snuffing

01:50:42.180 --> 01:50:45.832
and the negative health
consequences associated with those.

01:50:45.832 --> 01:50:47.760
I want to reemphasize that nicotine

01:50:47.760 --> 01:50:48.870
is not what causes cancer.

01:50:48.870 --> 01:50:51.450
It is the delivery
device that causes cancer

01:50:51.450 --> 01:50:53.490
and the other negative health effects.

01:50:53.490 --> 01:50:56.490
That is not to say that people
should be ingesting nicotine

01:50:56.490 --> 01:50:57.390
through any different methods

01:50:57.390 --> 01:50:58.890
simply to get a cognitive boost.

01:50:58.890 --> 01:51:00.720
There are certain
circumstances where that might

01:51:00.720 --> 01:51:03.480
be appropriate for the
occasional work about,

01:51:03.480 --> 01:51:06.030
certainly not for physical exercise,

01:51:06.030 --> 01:51:07.650
given what we talked about earlier,

01:51:07.650 --> 01:51:09.900
but of course there are
more and more approaches

01:51:09.900 --> 01:51:12.090
to increasing, not just nicotine,

01:51:12.090 --> 01:51:14.580
but acetylcholine generally
in order to achieve

01:51:14.580 --> 01:51:16.560
cognitive enhancement
or physical enhancement,

01:51:16.560 --> 01:51:19.350
or I should say physical
performance enhancement.

01:51:19.350 --> 01:51:20.490
Some of those we talked about earlier,

01:51:20.490 --> 01:51:22.050
such as Alpha GPC.

01:51:22.050 --> 01:51:24.720
In any event, nicotine,

01:51:24.720 --> 01:51:27.840
it should now be clear, is an
immensely powerful substance.

01:51:27.840 --> 01:51:30.000
One of the most commonly
ingested substances

01:51:30.000 --> 01:51:32.820
on the face of the earth and
has been for a very long time.

01:51:32.820 --> 01:51:35.340
And now that you understand
the underlying biology and the

01:51:35.340 --> 01:51:38.160
way in which this changes your
psychology and physiology,

01:51:38.160 --> 01:51:39.780
that should come as no surprise.

